hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2401","2020 - And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition to al-Lat and al-Uzza, and they raided a tribe of the Arabs and took captive their fighters and their offspring, and they said: O Messenger of Allah, they raided us without calling us; so he asked the people of the expedition and they confirmed them, and he said: Return them to their place of safety, then call them."
"2402","Chapter 26: Writing to the people of shirk before raiding them. 2021 - Abu Ya'la and al-Bazzar both said: Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Nuh ibn Qays narrated to us, from his brother Khalid ibn Qays, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Bakr ibn Wa'il: From Muhammad the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Bakr ibn Wa'il: Submit and you will be safe. They said: We found no one to read it except a man of Banu Dubay'ah, and they are called Banu al-Katib. Al-Bazzar said: We know it only by this chain, and Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"2403","Chapter 28: Warning against fleeing from the battle line. 2022 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Umar ibn Sa'id al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Tanukhi narrated to us, from Makhul, from Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with her), he said: She heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advising some of his family, he said: And do not flee on the day of battle."
"2404","Chapter 29: Dislike of payment for jihad. 2023 - Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, al-Wadin ibn Ata narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Marthad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it - meaning a hadith before it - its wording: I find nothing for him in this world and the Hereafter from his expedition except these dinars which were assigned to him; he said it to Ya'la ibn Munabbih in the story of a hired man of his."
"2405","Chapter 30: Emigration from the land of the enemy to the land of Islam. 2024 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Amr, from Busr ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Emigration remains as long as the polytheists are fought."
"2406","Chapter 31: No emigration after the conquest. 2025 - Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Bedouin life after emigration, and no emigration after the conquest."
"2407","Chapter 32: A slave does not fight except with his master's permission. 2026 - Al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayyah informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabi'ah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on one of his campaigns and passed by people of Muzaynah, and a slave of a woman among them followed him; when he was partway, he greeted him and he said to him: So-and-so? He said: Yes. He said: What is your matter? He said: I will fight with you. He said: Did your mistress permit you? He said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Return to her and convey greeting to her. So he returned to her and conveyed greeting to her and informed her, and she said: By Allah, did he command you to convey greeting to me? He said: Yes. She said: Return and fight with him."
"2408","Chapter 33: No jihad upon women. 2027/1 - Abu Bakr said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Salih, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, Sa'id ibn Amr al-Qurashi narrated to me, he said: Umm Kabshah, a woman of Uranah - Uranah of Quda'ah - said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to go out with such-and-such army? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. She said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not intend to fight; I only intend to treat the wounded and the sick - or: give drink to the sick. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that it would become a precedent, and that so-and-so woman went out, I would have permitted you; but stay."
"2409","2027/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"2410","Chapter 34: Making treaties with the people of shirk. 2028 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from al-Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, Juhaynah came to him and said to him: You have settled among us, so make a firm pact for us so that we are safe from you and you are safe from us. He said: So he made a firm pact for them, and they did not submit."
"2411","2029/1 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ubayd Allah narrated to us, al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Sufyan informed me, Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib - he is al-Kalbi - informed me, from Asbagh ibn Nubatah, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made peace with Banu Taghlib on condition that they remain upon their religion and not baptize their children into Christianity; and they broke it; and if my rule is completed, I will kill the fighters and take the offspring captive."
"2412","2029/2 - And Ubayd Allah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman al-Bakrawi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib al-Kalbi narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it, and its wording: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) make peace with the Christians of Banu Taghlib on condition that they not baptize their children; if they do, the covenant is absolved from them. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah they have done it; by Allah, if my rule is completed, I will surely kill their fighters and surely take their offspring captive."
"2413","Chapter 36: Nullity of the blood of one under pact who insults the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 2030 - Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: There was a Muslim man blind who used to stay with a Jewish woman, and she was good in her treatment toward him, but she used to insult the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when she mentioned him. So he forbade her and she refused to stop, so he killed her, and that was raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he asked him, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, she was among the best of people to me in treatment, but she used to insult you when she mentioned you, so I forbade her and she refused to stop, so I killed her; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared her blood void."
"2414","2031/1 - Hushaym narrated to us, Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from one who informed him, that Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was brought a monk and it was said to him: This one insults the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: If I had heard him I would have killed him; we did not give them the covenant for them to insult our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2415","2031/2 - And al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Husayn, he said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) passed by a monk... similar to it."
"2416","2032 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Harmalah ibn Imran, from Ka'b ibn Alqamah, he said: Gharafah ibn al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with him) - and he had companionship with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - passed by a man who wore every day a garment, or he said: a suit, unlike the other, wearing in the year three hundred and sixty garments; and he had a pact, so he called him to Islam and he became angry and insulted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so Gharafah killed him. Amr ibn al-As said to him: They only feel secure with us because of the pact. He said: We did not pact with them that they harm us concerning Allah Most High and His Messenger."
"2417","Chapter 37: Warning against breaking the pact. 2033/1 - Abu Bakr said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Bashir ibn Muhajir informed me, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people break the pact except killing is among them, and indecency never appears among a people except death is imposed upon them."
"2418","2033/2 - And al-Ruyanani said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us with it. This is a good chain."
"2419","Chapter 38: Protecting the people of the covenant and explanation of what nullifies their pact. 2034 - Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: A man of the people of the covenant prodded a Muslim woman's donkey, then grappled with her, and Awf ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) came between him and her and struck him; so he came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned that to him, so he called the woman and asked her and she confirmed Awf (may Allah be pleased with him); so he ordered him and he was crucified; then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O people, fear Allah concerning the covenant of Muhammad; do not wrong them; whoever of them does the like of this has no covenant."
"2420","Chapter 39: Prohibition of killing women, children, old men, servants and leaders. 2035 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a campaign and appointed Khalid ibn al-Walid over his vanguard; and he saw a slain woman and said: Who killed this? They said: Khalid ibn al-Walid killed her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Catch up with Khalid ibn al-Walid and say to him: Do not kill a woman or a child or a hired servant. And al-asif is the hired worker."
"2421","2036 - Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Aban, from al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - and the people had hastened in killing children on the day of Khaybar - and he became angry and said: I forbade you from killing children and the elderly. A man said: May my father and mother be for you, O Messenger of Allah, what is it to us if we kill the children of the polytheists? He said: And what do you know of what they would have done?"
"2422","2037 - And with it to Abu Ishaq, from Sufyan, from Abu Fazarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a slain woman on the day of Khaybar and said: Who killed this? A man said: I, O Messenger of Allah; I mounted her behind me and she tried to kill me, so I pushed her away; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her burial. And the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Unays will come, Allah Most High willing, in the story of the killing of Ibn Abi al-Huqayq in the campaigns."
"2423","Chapter 40: Sincere advice to the leader. 2038/1 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice. They said: To whom, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To the Book of Allah, to His Prophet, and to the leaders of the Muslims."
"2424","2038/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this. This chain is good, except that it is defective; and what is preserved is what Ibn Uyaynah narrated from Amr ibn Dinar from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim from Abu Salih from Ata ibn Yazid from Tamim al-Dari (may Allah be pleased with him); and the hadith of Tamim (may Allah be pleased with him) is in Sahih Muslim."
"2425","Chapter 41: Protection of a Muslim, even a woman or child. 2039 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from al-Walid ibn Abi Malik, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: One man among them grants protection on behalf of the Muslims."
"2426","Chapter 42: Fulfillment of the pact. 2040/1 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri informing from Ibn Suraqah or Ibn Akhi Suraqah. Sufyan said: And Wa'il ibn Dawud informed me part of it from al-Zuhri, and I do not distinguish what I memorized from al-Zuhri and what Wa'il informed me. Suraqah said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he was at al-Ji'ranah, and I did not pass a detachment of the detachments of the Ansar except they said: Away, away; and when I reached him - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I raised the writing and said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had written safety for me in a scrap - meaning when he emigrated. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; today is a day of fulfillment, righteousness and truthfulness."
"2427","2040/2 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Suraqah ibn Malik, from Ibn Akhi Suraqah, from Suraqah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Ji'ranah, and I did not pass a detachment of the detachments of the Ansar except it struck my head and they said: Away, away, until I reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and when I saw him I said: I am, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; today is a day of fulfillment, righteousness and truthfulness. Sufyan said: By his saying: I am, he meant: the owner of the safety which you wrote for me in the scrap; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had written safety for him in a scrap when he met him on the day he emigrated with Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to the cave."
"2428","Chapter 43: Prohibition of mutilation. 2041 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Shu'aythi narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade mutilation."
"2429","2042 - Al-Harith said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Makhul, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade his armies from mutilating any of the disbelievers."
"2430","Chapter 44: Guarding. 2043 - Ishaq said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us, al-Awwam ibn Hawshab informed me, a shaykh who was guarding the frontier at the coast narrated to me, he said: I went out on my night of guard duty; none of those upon whom guarding was due went out except me; so I came to the building and ascended it - and the building is the place of guarding - and it began to appear to me that the sea was looming until it aligned with the tops of the mountains, and that happened repeatedly while I was awake; then I slept and saw in sleep as if a banner was with me, and the people of Madinah were walking behind me while I was before them; and when morning came I returned to Madinah and met the commander of the army and Abu Salih the freed slave of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) - and they were the first who went out from Madinah - and they said to me: Where are the people? I said: They returned before me. They said: Why do you not tell us the truth; we are the first who went out from Madinah. He said: So I informed them that no one went out except me. Abu Salih said: What did you see? I said: By Allah, it appeared to me in what I saw that the sea loomed until it aligned with the tops of the mountains. Abu Salih said: You spoke truth; Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: There is no night except the sea looms three times over the people of the earth, seeking permission from Allah Most High to pour over them - meaning to flood - and Allah Most High restrains it. I said: And I also saw in sleep as if the banner was with me, and the people of Madinah were walking with me while I was before them. So Abu Salih said: If your dream is true, you will win the reward of this city tonight. He said: And Abu Salih used to keep away from me before that, but it was as if he trusted me, so he began narrating to me, and said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) enjoined upon us that three share: a man sells for us, a man fights, and a man brings provisions for us; so this is my turn, and I am now returning to Madinah. I said: Ahmad narrated only the raised part of it from Yazid."
"2431","2044 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Ata, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Two eyes the Fire will not touch: an eye that overflowed from fear of Allah, and an eye that spent the night guarding in the path of Allah."
"2432","2045 - And Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Shabib ibn Bishr, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two eyes the Fire will never touch: an eye that spent the night guarding the Muslims in the path of Allah Most High, and an eye that wept from fear of Allah Most High."
"2433","2046 - And Abd ibn Humayd said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said - and Allah knows best -: Forbidden for two eyes to be touched by the Fire: an eye that wept from fear of Allah, and an eye that spent the night guarding Islam against the people of disbelief."
"2434","2047 - And Ishaq said: Sufyan informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Umm Mubashshir: She asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which people are highest in station with Allah? He said: A man upon the back of his horse frightening the enemy and they frightening him. The hadith is mursal."
"2435","Chapter 45: Ruling on land taken by the people of shirk. 2048/1 - Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, from al-Wazir ibn Abd Allah al-Khawlani, from al-Zubaydi - and he is Muhammad ibn al-Walid - from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever the polytheists grant land has no land."
"2436","2048/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Talqani narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us with it."
"2437","Chapter 46: Food found in enemy land. 2049 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ashja'i, from Abu Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Khaybar: Eat and feed the animals, but do not carry away."
"2438","Chapter 47: Prohibition of using spoils before division. 2050 - Abu Bakr said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Musaffar, from al-Hasan, Thabit ibn Rufay' narrated to me - and he used to be appointed over expeditions -: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Beware of misappropriation: a man marries a woman before division then returns her to the division, or wears a garment until it wears out then returns it to the division."
"2439","Chapter 48: Stipends and the ruling on what remains of them. 2051/1 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, my father informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: When Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed a prayer, he would sit for the people for whoever had a need; and if none had a need, he stood and entered. He said: Then he prayed prayers in which he did not sit for the people. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: So I came to the door and said: O Yarfa, is the Commander of the Believers ill? He said: The Commander of the Believers has no complaint. So I sat, then Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) came and sat, then Yarfa came out and said: Stand, O Ibn Affan; stand, O Ibn Abbas. So they entered upon Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and before him were piles of wealth, upon each pile a shoulder blade. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I looked among the people of Madinah and found you two of the most numerous of its people in clan, so take this wealth and divide it; and whatever is surplus, return it. He said: As for Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), he scooped; and as for me, I knelt on my knees and said: Even if there is a shortfall, will you make it up to us? He said: A trait from a rough one - meaning a stone from a mountain - was this not with Allah when Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions ate dried skin? I said: Yes, by Allah, this was with Allah Mighty and Majestic while Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive; and had it been opened for him, he would have done with it other than what you do. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) became angry and said: Tell me, what would he have done? I said: Then he would have eaten and fed us. He said: So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sobbed until his ribs shook, then said: I wish I emerge from it even, neither for me nor against me."
"2440","2051/2 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us with this. And it will come, Allah Most High willing, in the virtue of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), some things of this."
"2441","Chapter 49: Land grants. 2052 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Ja'far, he said: al-Abbas came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted me Bahrain as a fief. He said: Who testified for you? He said: al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah; and he mentioned the hadith. In it is a break."
"2442","2053 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih informed us, Yahya ibn Amr ibn Yahya ibn Salamah al-Hudhali narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Qays ibn Malik al-Arhabi: In Your name, O Allah; from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Qays ibn Malik: Peace upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings and His forgiveness; as for what follows, I appoint you over your people, their town-dwellers and their non-Arabs, their freedmen, their majority and their outskirts; and I grant you as fief two hundred sa's of the grain of Yasar, and two hundred sa's of the raisins of Khaywan, valid for you and your descendants after you forever and ever. Qays said: The saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - forever and ever - is dearer to me; I hope my descendants remain forever. Yahya said: His saying: their town-dwellers means the people of the desert, and their majority means the people of the villages. I said: This hadith is rejected, and the strangest in it is his saying: He wrote: In Your name, O Allah."
"2443","2054 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Wa'il Khalid ibn Muhammad al-Basri narrated to us, Fahd ibn Awf the freed slave of Banu Amir narrated to us, Na'il ibn Mutarrif ibn Razin al-Aslami Abu Anas narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather Razin ibn Anas, he said: When Islam appeared, we had a well and I feared those around us would overpower us over it, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he wrote for me a writing: From Muhammad the Messenger of Allah; as for what follows, they have their well if he is truthful, and they have their house if he is truthful. He said: We did not present it in judgment to any judge of Madinah except he judged for us by it. He said: In the writing was a spelling: it was kawun. This is a strange hadith, Fahd alone narrated it; al-Fallas said: Abandoned. Al-Tabarani narrated it elevated from Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Fahd; and Ibn Mandah from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yahya from Abu Mas'ud al-Razi from Fahd; and al-Tabari and others from the route of Muhammad ibn Humayd from Na'il ibn Mutarrif ibn al-Abbas from his father from his grandfather, he said: I sought from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a well as fief - the hadith; and Allah knows best which of them is correct. Ibn Mandah said: Yahya ibn Yunus al-Shirazi narrated it from Abd al-Salam ibn Umar al-Husayni from Na'il ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Hazm ibn Anas ibn Amir al-Aslami, my father narrated to me, from Lubabah, he said: The writing was written by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Razin ibn Anas."
"2444","2055 - And Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Dinar informed us, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah, he said: Uyaynah ibn Hisn and al-Aqra' ibn Habis came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: O successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we have a salty barren land with no pasture nor benefit in it; if you see fit to grant it to us? He said: So he granted it to them both and wrote for them a writing for it. And he mentioned the hadith, and it is in the chapter of ministers in the Book of Leadership."
"2445","2056 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Walid ibn Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil informed us, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Hurrah al-Asadi narrated to me, from his father - and he was of the most knowledgeable of people about the Sawad - he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) as judge, and wrote to Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him) with ten qualities; I memorized six and forgot four: Do not grant except the wealth of Kisra or his household, or one killed in battle, or the houses of cloaks, or the place of prisons, and the outlets of water and thickets."
"2446","Chapter 50: Whoever accepts Islam upon something, it is his. 2057 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Jamil narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Yasin al-Zayyat, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever accepts Islam while possessing something, it is his."
"2447","2058 - And al-Harith said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Hajjaj informed me, from Abu Sa'id al-A'sha, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning a slave, when he accepts Islam and then his master comes and accepts Islam, that he is more entitled to him."
"2448","Chapter 51: Jizyah and truce. Hadith 91: The hadith of Abd Allah ibn Shaddad (may Allah be pleased with him) about the letter to Heraclius will come, Allah Most High willing, in the campaigns. 2059/1 - And Musaddad said: Fudayl narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The people of idols are fought over prayer, and the People of the Book are fought over the jizyah."
"2449","2059/2 - And Waki' said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us the like of it."
"2450","2060 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah, Qatadah informed us, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf, he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stipulated upon the people of the covenant hospitality of a day and night, and that they not neglect the bridges; and if a Muslim man is killed in their land, his blood money is upon them."
"2451","2061 - And Abu Bakr said: Abdah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Aslam the freed slave of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the officials of the jizyah not to impose the jizyah upon women and children; and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to seal the people of the jizyah on their necks."
"2452","2062/1 - Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the Magians of Hajar presenting Islam; whoever accepted, it was accepted from him; and whoever refused, the jizyah was imposed upon him, on condition that none of their women be married, nor any of their slaughtered animals be eaten."
"2453","2062/2 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, similar to it."
"2454","2063/1 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Sa'd - he is al-Baqqal - narrated to me, from Nasr ibn Asim, he said: Farwah ibn Nawfal al-Ashja'i said: On what basis is jizyah taken from the Magians when they are not People of the Book? So al-Mustawrid (may Allah be pleased with him) stood to him and seized him by his collar and said: O enemy of Allah, do you impugn Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)? And he took him to the palace; then Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to them and said: Al-bada' - Sufyan said: meaning: Sit - so they sat in the shade of the palace, and he informed him of his saying; so Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I am the most knowledgeable of people about the Magians: they had a knowledge they taught and a book they studied; and their king became drunk one day and fell upon his daughter or his sister, and some of his kingdom's people saw him; and when he sobered up, they came to impose the prescribed punishment upon him, but he refused them and called the people of his kingdom and said: Do you know a religion better than the religion of Adam? And Adam used to marry his sons to his daughters, and I am upon the religion of Adam; so what makes you averse to his religion? So they pledged him and fought those who opposed them until they were killed; and in the morning their book was taken up by night, lifted from among them, and the knowledge in their breasts departed; so they are People of the Book; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) took the jizyah from them."
"2455","2063/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Abu Sa'd, and he mentioned it abridged."
"2456","Chapter 52: Division of fay' and spoils. 2064 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Mas'udi informed us, from al-Qasim, he said: Abd Allah said: By the One besides whom there is no god, Allah Most High distributed this fay' upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before Persia and Rome were opened."
"2457","2065/1 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, a man of Balqayn narrated to me, that a man came to the Prophet while he was crossing the valley of al-Qura and said: O Muhammad, to what do you call us? He said: To Allah alone. He said: Is this wealth - is anyone more entitled to it than another? He said: A fifth is for Allah Most High, and four fifths for these; and if you pull an arrow from your quiver, you are not more entitled to it than your brother. He said: Who are these - meaning those who earned anger? He said: The Jews. He said: And who are these - meaning those astray? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Christians."
"2458","2065/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, from a man of Balqayn, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Wadi al-Qura, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what have you been commanded? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded that you worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and that you establish prayer and give zakat. I said: O Messenger of Allah, who are these - meaning those who earned anger? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Jews. I said: And who are these - meaning those astray? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Christians. I said: To whom belongs the spoils, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And he mentioned it."
"2459","Chapter 53: Share of near relatives. 2066 - Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Salih the freed slave of Umm Hani - and his name is Badhan - from Umm Hani bint Abi Talib, she said: Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to ask him for the share of near relatives; so Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to her: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The share of near relatives is theirs during my life, and not theirs after my death. I said: This wording they did not bring; and Ibn al-Sa'ib is al-Kalbi, abandoned."
"2460","Chapter 54: Shares applying to what is sold for gold or silver. 2067 - Musaddad said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Ibn Awn informed us, from Khalid ibn Durayk, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, he said: People want to turn me away from my religion, and by Allah I hope I remain upon it until I die: there is nothing sold for gold or silver except in it is the fifth of Allah and the shares of the Muslims."
"2461","Chapter 55: Clarification that extra spoils were shared by whoever took them before division was revealed. 2068 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Yahya ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, from al-Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, he sent us and commanded us to raid a tribe of Kinanah; and the fay' at that time was: whoever took something, it was his."
"2462","Chapter 56: Division of fay' for emigrants and for those in whose land it occurred. 2069 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani, he said: I witnessed Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Jabiyah; he praised Allah and extolled Him as He deserves, then said: As for what follows, this fay' Allah bestowed upon you; the noble and the lowly in it are alike; none is more entitled to it than another, except for these two tribes: Lakhm and Judham; for I will not divide anything for them. So a man of Lakhm stood and said: O Ibn al-Khattab, I adjure you by Allah concerning justice. He said: Ibn al-Khattab intends only justice and equality; by Allah, I know: had the emigration been at San'a, none of Lakhm and Judham would have gone out to it except few; so I will not make those who bore travel and bought mounts like a people who fought only in their own lands. So Abu Hudayrij stood and said: O Commander of the Believers, if Allah Most High drove the emigration to us in our lands and we aided it and believed it, is that what removes our right in Islam? So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: By Allah I will surely divide - three times - then he divided among the people; each man received half a dinar, and when his wife was with him, he gave him a dinar, and when his grandfather was with him, he gave him half a dinar."
"2463","Chapter 57: Return of spoils before division. 2070 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Qutaylah bint Jumay' narrated to me, Yazid ibn Hunayf narrated to me, from his father, he said: I heard Umarah ibn Ahmar al-Mazini, one of Banu Rabi'ah ibn Mazin, saying: I was among camels of mine pasturing them in the pre-Islamic time, and the cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or the cavalry of his Companions raided them; so I gathered my camels and mounted the stallion, and it spread its legs and urinated; so I dismounted from it and mounted a she-camel among them, and escaped upon it while they drove the camels; so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he returned them to me, and he had not yet divided them."
"2464","2071 - Musaddad said: Yahya - he is Ibn Sa'id - narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid informed us, from Ibn Awn, from al-Harith ibn Qays, from al-Azhar ibn Yazid al-Rahawi, he said: A slave girl fled and joined the enemy, and the Muslims took her as spoils; and the Muradiyyun recognized her, so they came to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Our slave girl fled from us. He said: I have no knowledge in this, but I will write to the Commander of the Believers, so wait for his letter; so the Muradiyyun fell silent a while; then he said: The letter of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) has come to me concerning your slave girl: If it has been made fifth and divided, then that is its course; otherwise return it to its people. They said: By Allah, did Umar write that? He said: By Allah, and it is not lawful for me to lie."
"2465","Chapter 58: Spoils of the slayer for the killer. 2072 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us, Ibn Abi Sabrah informed us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to take a fifth from the salb. This is mursal, weak."
"2466","Chapter of the Spoils (59) (2073/1). Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Makhul: Hajjaj ibn Abd Allah al-Nasri narrated to us, he said: Spoils are a right; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave spoils. (2073/2). Al-Baghawi said in his Mu'jam: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with it."
"2467","(2073/3). And Abu Nu'aym said in al-Ma'rifah: Muhammad ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman - meaning Ibn Abi Shaybah - narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Ya'ish narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ya'la narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, Makhul narrated to us, al-Hajjaj narrated to us with it."
"2468","(2073/4). And al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said in his Musnad: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with it."
"2469","(2073/5). And al-Bawardi and al-Tabarani said: Mutayyan narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, with it. And the hadith is defective (ma'lul)."
"2470","(2073/6). Ibn A'idh said in al-Maghazi: al-Walid narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Tamim, and Hafs ibn Ghaylan narrated to me, that they heard Makhul narrating, he said: On the Day of Badr, a party of the Muslims fought, and a party held firm with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then the party that had fought came with the spoils and things they had taken, and the booty was divided among them ... then he mentioned the story. Makhul said: al-Hajjaj ibn Suhayl al-Basri narrated this hadith to me, and nothing prevented me from asking him about its chain except awe of him. Our shaykh (Ibn Hajar) said: This indicates that it is not from a Companion, and that his father's name is Suhayl, not Abd Allah."
"2471","Chapter of Conciliating Hearts upon Islam (60) (2074). Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Nasr ibn Asim al-Laythi, from a man among them, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and embraced Islam on condition that he pray two prayers, and he accepted it from him."
"2472","Chapter of the Imam Preferring Some of the Subjects with the Consent of the Rest (61) (2075). Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Husayn narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from Dhakwan the freedman of A'ishah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: A case (durj) was brought by Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him); his companions looked at it and did not know its value, so he said: Do you permit me to send it to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) because of the love of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for her? They said: Yes. So it was brought to A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she opened it, and it was said: This Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) has sent to you. She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: What has Umar ibn al-Khattab opened upon me after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? O Allah, do not keep me alive for next year's gift."
"2473","Chapter of the Dislike of the Imam Taking Something from the Booty for Himself Before Division (62) (2076). Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating, from Hanash, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Emigrants acquired a tent of leather after Hunayn or on the Day of Hunayn, so the Emigrants said: O Prophet of Allah, we have willingly given it to you; take it to shade yourself in, and some of us will shade with you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Would you like your Prophet to be in a tent of fire?"
"2474","Chapter of Kindness to the Orphans of the Warriors (63) (2077). Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn Hubab narrated to us, Ayyash ibn Uqbah narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Umayyah al-Damri narrated to me, Ibn Umm al-Hakam narrated to me, my mother Umm al-Hakam narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from one of his expeditions having taken captives; she and her sister went until they entered upon Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her), and they went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and she asked him to speak to them and complained to him of need. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The orphans of the people of Badr have preceded you. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Abu Dawud brought it out, but he said from al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan, from Umm al-Hakam Duba'ah bint al-Zubayr."
"2475","Chapter of the Gravity of Ghulul (64) (2078). Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Yahsibi narrated to me, my father narrated to me, from Habib ibn Maslamah, who said: I heard Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If my Ummah does not commit ghulul, no enemy will ever overpower them. So Abu Dharr said to Habib ibn Maslamah: Has the enemy held firm against you for the milking of a sheep? He said: Yes, and of three goats with narrow udders. So Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You have committed ghulul, by the Lord of the Ka'bah. Ishaq said: Al-uzuz is narrowness of the teat."
"2476","(2079/1). Abu Ya'la said: Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abu al-Khattab, from Abu Idris, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing of the spoils of the Muslims is lawful for anyone, little or much, neither thread nor needle, for the taker nor the giver, except rightfully."
"2477","(2079/2). Khalifah ibn Khayyat narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Layth mentioning from Abu Idris, from Thawban who said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lawful for me of these spoils? He said: Neither thread nor needle is lawful of it, neither for the taker nor the giver."
"2478","(2080). Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah al-Ash'ari narrated to us, Hafs ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am holding you by your waistbands from the Fire; come away from the Fire, but you overpower me, plunging into it like the plunging of moths and grasshoppers. I am about to release your waistbands and precede you at the Hawd, and you will come to me together and in groups, and I will recognize you by your marks and names as a man recognizes the strange camel among his camels; and you will be taken to the left, and I will plead for you with the Lord of the worlds, saying: O my Lord, my people; O my Lord, my Ummah. He will say: O Muhammad, you do not know what they innovated after you; they kept walking backwards upon their heels after you. Let me not recognize one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a sheep that bleats, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad; and I will say: I own nothing for you; I conveyed to you. Let me not recognize one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a horse that neighs, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad; and I will say: I own nothing for you; I conveyed to you. And let me not recognize one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a leather water-skin, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad; and I will say: I own nothing for you; I conveyed to you."
"2479","Chapter of Prohibiting the Sale of Shares Before They Are Divided (65) (2081). Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, al-Qasim and Makhul narrated to us, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade on the Day of Khaybar that shares be sold until they are divided."
"2480","Chapter of Ransoming Captives (66) (2082). Ishaq said: Abd Allah ibn Idris informed us, he said: I heard Asim ibn Kulayb narrating from his father, who said: We came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was in his tent, and I called out: I am so-and-so son of so-and-so al-Jarmi, and a nephew of ours is a captive among the captives of so-and-so, and I have presented to them the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and we used to say that the ruling is four. Ibn Idris said: They are unah, meaning captives taken in the pre-Islamic period. This is a good (hasan) hadith."
"2481","(2083). Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Yahya ibn Yahya al-Ghassani, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) ruled concerning what the Arabs competed over of ransom: four hundred."
"2482","(2084). Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash informed us, Abu Husayn narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There is no ownership over an Arab, and we shall not take from a man's hand a captive who embraced Islam in his possession, but we shall value him at five camels."
"2483","(2085). Hafs ibn Ghiyath informed us, from Abu Salamah, that is Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me when he was stabbed: Know that every captive among the believers in the hands of the polytheists, his ransom is from the treasury of the Muslims. Its chain is good (hasan)."
"2484","(2086). Amr ibn Muhammad informed us, Asbat ibn Nasr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abd Khayr who said: We raided with Salman ibn Rabi'ah to Balanjar and besieged its people; while we were so, Salman was struck with a stone that hit his head, and he said: If I die, bury me at the foot of this city. He died and we buried him where he said. Then we besieged its people and conquered the city and took captives and much wealth, and a man among us took a thousand dirhams or more. When we returned we reached a place called al-Sadd, and we could not pass through it until we went into the sea, and we came out upon Muqan, Jilan and al-Daylam; we would pass no people but they asked us for peace and gave us pledges, until the people here, meaning in Kufah, despaired of us and wept for us, and the poets spoke about us. He said: Abd Allah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) bought a Jewess for seven hundred dirhams; when he passed by the head of the Jalut he stayed with him, and Abd Allah said: O head of the Jalut, would you like an old woman of your people to buy her from me? He said: Yes. He said: I took her for seven hundred dirhams. He said: And for you a profit of seven hundred dirhams. He said: I said: No. He said: Then I have no need of her. I said: By Allah, you shall surely take her for what she cost or you shall disbelieve in your religion which you follow. He said: By Allah, I shall never buy her from you for anything. He said: Abd Allah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Come near; so he came near, and he recited to him what is in the Torah: You will not find an owned person among the Children of Israel but you shall buy him for what he cost and free him. He said: And if they come to you as captives, you shall ransom them, though driving them out was forbidden to you; do you believe in part of the Book and disbelieve in part ... the verse. He said: By Allah, I shall surely buy her from you for what she cost. He said: I had sworn not to let her go for less than four thousand dirhams. He said: So he brought him four thousand dirhams, and he returned two thousand dirhams to him and took two thousand. Abd Khayr said: When I arrived I came to al-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym to greet him, and he had acquired many slaves. He said: So he recited: You shall never attain righteousness until you spend of what you love. And he freed them. This is a good (hasan) chain."
"2485","Chapter of Stratagem and Deception in War (67) (2087). Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought some people of the Zutt - I think he killed them. Then he looked to the sky, then looked to the earth, and said: Allah is Most Great, Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth; dig this place - no, rather this place. Then he looked to the sky, then looked to the earth and said: Allah is Most Great, Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth; dig this place. He said: So they dug and threw them into it, then he entered, and I entered upon him and said: Tell me about what you were doing just now - did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenant to you anything concerning them? He said: For me to fall from the sky to the earth is dearer to me than to say about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he did not say; I was only using stratagem. Tell me, if I had said: Allah is Most Great, Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth, dig this place - what would it have been? Sahih."
"2486","(2088). Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Husayn al-Ashqar narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Sawwar Abu Idris, from al-Musayyab ibn Najabah who said: We entered upon al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: War is deception. Muhammad ibn Sa'id followed him up, from Abd Allah ibn Bukayr; al-Bazzar brought it out."
"2487","(2089). And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Yasir Ammar narrated to us, Hisham Abu al-Miqdam narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: War is deception."
"2488","Book of the Caliphate and Leadership (21) (2090). Abu Ya'la said: Ubayd Allah - that is al-Qawariri - narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abd Allah ibn Subay' who said: It was said to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): Will you not appoint a successor? He said: No, but I leave you to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left you to."
"2489","(2091). Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abd al-Jalil - that is Ibn Atiyyah - narrated to us, Abu Mijlaz narrated to us, he said: Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) lay down in a garden among the gardens of Madinah ... then he mentioned a story; and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Whom will you appoint as successor after me? A man among the people said to him: al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: Then you would appoint a miserly, ill-tempered man - meaning bad in character. A man said: We shall appoint Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: How can you appoint a man whose first gift from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was land he gifted to him, and he put it in the dowry of a Jewess? A man among the people said: We shall appoint Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: By my life, you will not appoint him; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if you appointed him he would establish you upon the truth even if you disliked it. He said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We know the caliph after you. He sat up and said: Who? He said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) - and al-Walid was the maternal brother of Uthman. He said: Then what of the love of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) for wealth and his kindness to his household?"
"2490","(2092/1). Jarir - that is Ibn Abd al-Hamid - informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit al-Jumahi, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with them both) would converse privately between themselves. I said to them: Have you not memorized anything of the counsel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So they began recalling it, and they said: The beginning of this Ummah is prophethood and mercy, then there shall be caliphate and mercy, then there shall be biting kingship, then there shall be insolence, tyranny and corruption in the Ummah; they shall deem wines and private parts lawful, and corruption in the Ummah; they shall be aided in that and provided for until they meet Allah, Mighty and Majestic. This is a good (hasan) hadith."
"2491","(2092/2). Al-Tayalisi narrated it: from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Layth, similar to it."
"2492","(2092/3). And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir - that is Ibn Abd al-Hamid - narrated to us -"
"2493","(2092/4). He said: And Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Layth, with it."
"2494","(2093/1). And Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us. (2093/2). And Ishaq said: Abu Usamah informed us; both said: al-Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq who said: A man came to Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Did your Prophet narrate to you how many caliphs there will be after him? He said: Yes, and no one before you asked me about it, and you are among the youngest of the people in age. He said: They will be the number of the chiefs of Musa: twelve chiefs. This is a good (hasan) chain."
"2495","(2093/3). And Abu Ya'la said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Mujalid with it."
"2496","(2093/1). And Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from al-Sha'bi, from his uncle Qays ibn Abd, who said: A bedouin came to Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him). Then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not say: among the youngest of the people in age."
"2497","(2094). And Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Simak ibn Salamah, from Tamim ibn Hadlam who said: The first one to be greeted with leadership at Kufah was al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah (may Allah be pleased with him); he disliked it then approved it. Sahih."
"2498","Chapter of the Dislike of Leadership for One Unable to Bear It (1) (2095). Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus and Jarir informed us, from al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Maysarah, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Rafi' ibn Abi Rafi' al-Ta'i who said: When the expedition of Dhat al-Salasil took place, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army and appointed over them Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him), and among them was Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him); it is the expedition about which the people of Sham boast, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) over an army in which was Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he commanded them to call to arms whoever of the Muslims they passed by. They passed by us in our abode and called us to arms, so we went forth with them. I said: I shall choose for myself a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to serve him and learn from him, for I cannot come to Madinah whenever I wish. So I chose Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and accompanied him. He had a Fadak cloak which we would spread over him when he rode, and we would all wear it when we camped; it is the cloak with which Hawazin taunted him, saying: Shall we follow this cloaked one after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? When we finished our expedition we returned, and I had not asked him about anything. I said to him: I have accompanied you and I have a right upon you, and I have not asked you about anything; teach me what will benefit me, for I cannot come to Madinah every Saturday. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: That was in my mind before you mentioned it to me: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, establish the prescribed prayer, give the obligatory zakat, perform Hajj to the House, fast Ramadan, and do not take command over two men. I said: As for prayer and zakat I know them, but as for leadership, people only obtain good through leadership. He said: You have asked me to strive for you, so I have striven for you: People entered Islam willingly and unwillingly, so Allah granted them refuge from wrong; they are the seekers of Allah's refuge, the neighbors of Allah, under the protection of Allah; whoever wrongs any of them only betrays his Lord. By Allah, if the sheep of one's neighbor or his camel is taken, he remains the rest of his lifetime angry for his neighbor, and Allah stands behind His neighbor. When we returned to our homes and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away and the people pledged allegiance to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was made caliph, I said: Who was made caliph after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Your companion Abu Bakr. So I came to Madinah and kept seeking him until I found him alone; I took his hand and said: Do you not know me? I am your companion. He said: Yes. I said: Do you not remember what you said to me - do not take command over two men - yet you have taken command over the people? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while the people were fresh from pre-Islamic ignorance, and my companions burdened me, and I feared they would apostatize. By Allah, he kept excusing himself until I excused him. And Jarir added in it, he said: I used to herd sheep in the pre-Islamic period, and matters kept on with me until I became a headman under the rule of al-Hajjaj - Rafi' ibn Abi Rafi' al-Ta'i says it. This is a strange (gharib) hadith; Sulayman, the shaykh of al-A'mash, I have not yet known him; and Ahmad narrated part of it with another chain. (92) And the hadith of Hibban ibn Buh al-Suda'i - there is no good in leadership for a Muslim man - shall come, if Allah wills, in the chapter of the signs of prophethood. (93) And likewise the hadith of Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Suda'i."
"2499","(2096). And Abu Bakr said: Abdah narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym, from Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Suda'i who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no good in leadership for a believing man."
"2500","(2097). And Musaddad said: Bishr - that is Ibn al-Mufaddal - narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Ishaq who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad (may Allah be pleased with him) on an expedition; when he returned he said: How did you find your expedition? He said: I kept on until I thought those with me were my servants; by Allah, I shall never work over two men as long as I live."
"2501","(2098). And Ishaq said: Abu Nu'aym al-Mula'i informed us, Badr ibn Uthman narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Hafs ibn Umar ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to me, from Sa'd, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), that he wanted to employ a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the man disliked that, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) grew angry and said: This matter we are engaged in must have helpers for it. When he saw that, he conceded to him and said: I shall go to my family to counsel them, then return. He said: Yes. He left him and met his uncle, who said: I command you not to do so. He said: Then what of his command? He said: Go in the evening and I shall go with you; when he sees you he will say to you: Have you not gone in the evening? So say: O Commander of the Faithful, I ask you to excuse me. He did so, and he said: Who forbade you? He said: So-and-so - for his uncle. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - when he wanted to employ a man over something of the Muslims' affairs, and the man said: O Messenger of Allah, I ask you to excuse me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I choose for you to sit back, for no man shall ever be put in command over ten but he shall come to Allah the Exalted bound on the Day of Resurrection, until his deed is what releases him. And Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was reclining, so he sat upright and kept calling out: What deed releases him? He called out with that repeatedly."
"2502","(2099/1). And Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, from Muhammad al-Rasibi, from Bishr ibn Asim who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him about his appointment, and he said: I have no need of it; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The rulers shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection and made to stand upon the bridge of Hell; whoever was obedient to Allah, Allah shall take him by his right hand until He saves him, and whoever was disobedient to Allah, the bridge shall break with him into a valley of blazing fire. He said: So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sent to Abu Dharr and to Salman (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said to Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him): Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by Allah; and beyond the valley is another valley of fire. He said: And he asked Salman (may Allah be pleased with him), but he disliked to inform him of anything. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Who will take it with what it entails? Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One whose eye and nose Allah has taken and whose cheek is laid to the ground."
"2503","(2099/2). And al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with it."
"2504","(2099/3). And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Hashraj ibn Nubatah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Habib, from Bishr ibn Asim, from his father who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) sent to him to seek his help over some charity, but he refused to work for him. He said: Why? He said: Because I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When the Day of Resurrection comes, the ruler shall be brought and cast upon the bridge of Hell; Allah the Exalted shall command the bridge, and it shall jolt him with a jolt that dislodges every bone of his from its place; then Allah the Exalted shall command the bones to return to their places. If he was obedient to Allah, He shall take his hand and give him two shares of His mercy; if he was disobedient to Allah, the bridge shall break with him and he shall fall into Hell for seventy years. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: You heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we did not hear; and Salman and Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with them both) were sitting. Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Yes, by Allah, O Umar - with the seventy, seventy autumns in a valley of blazing fire. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said, with his hand upon his forehead: To Allah we belong and to Him we return; who will take it with what it entails? He said: One whose nose Allah has taken and whose cheek is stuck to the ground."
"2505","(2099/4). And Abd said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Ayzar informed us, from a man of the people of Sham who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wanted to employ Bishr ibn Asim, and he said: I shall not work for you. He said: Why? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The ruler shall be brought and made to stand upon the Sirat, and it shall shake with him until every limb of his is dislodged from its place; if he was just he shall pass, and if he was unjust he shall fall into the Fire for seventy autumns. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) entered the mosque with changed color, and Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: What is the matter with you, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: A hadith Bishr ibn Asim narrated to me. He said: And what is it? So he narrated it to him. Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Yes, I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Then who would desire office after this? Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: One whose nose Allah the Exalted has taken and whose cheek is laid low. Ibn Mandah said: The saying of one who said in it - from Bishr ibn Asim, from his father - is a mistake, not sound. And Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated it, from Abu Wa'il, from Bishr ibn Asim; thus al-Tabarani and others brought it out. And Ata narrated it, from Abd Allah ibn Sufyan, from Bishr ibn Asim; Ibn Mandah brought it out through his path. So these are chains, some strengthening others."
"2506","(2100). And Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar ibn Hafs, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he employed Sa'd ibn Ubadah; then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to greet him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Beware, O Sa'd, lest you come on the Day of Resurrection carrying upon your neck a camel that bellows. He said: O Messenger of Allah, if I do so, will that surely be? He said: Yes. He said: You know, O Prophet of Allah, that I am asked and I give; so excuse me, and he excused him."
"2507","Chapter of the Caliphate among Quraysh (2) (2101). Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and others - joining the hadith, from Urwah, who said: Musaylimah wrote to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): From Musaylimah ibn Habib to Muhammad the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Peace upon you; as for what follows: Quraysh has half the land, and we have half the land, but Quraysh are a transgressing people. And the two men bore witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and they said: Musaylimah does not deny that, except that he has been made a partner with you in the matter, and prophethood alongside your prophethood has newly come to him ... the hadith. In it there is a break (irsal)."
"2508","(2102). And Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Mubashshir narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Attab who said: Mu'awiyah stood upon the pulpit and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: People follow Quraysh in this matter; their best in pre-Islamic times are their best in Islam when they understand religion; and were it not that Quraysh would exult, I would inform them of what they have with Allah. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Ahmad narrated with this chain: The best women who rode camels are the women of Quraysh ... the hadith."
"2509","(2103). And Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Kathir ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: O company of Quraysh, you are the rulers after me over this matter ... And die not except as Muslims, and hold fast all together to the rope of Allah - to the end of the verse; and preserve me in regard to the Ansar, their sons, and the sons of their sons. Kathir is weak."
"2510","(2104). And with it he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: The ally of a people is among them, and the son of the sister of a people is among them."
"2511","(2105). And Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah narrated to us, Hafs ibn Khalid narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people one day and said: Verily the rulers are from Quraysh; verily the rulers are from Quraysh, so long as they uphold three things: when they judge they are just, when they covenant they fulfill, and when mercy is sought from them they show mercy; whoever among them does not do that, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people."
"2512","(2106). And Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Salih ibn Qudamah al-Jumahi narrated to us, Harun ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Abi Qutaylah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from his uncle Abd Allah ibn Urwah who said: The year of drought brought us Nabighah ibn Ja'dah, while we were with Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) at Makkah. He stood after praying Fajr with the people in the Sacred Mosque and spoke; Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Hold back, O Abu Layla, for poetry is the least of your means against us. As for the choicest of our wealth, it is for the family of al-Zubayr; as for its surplus, the Banu Asad keep us busy from you. But you have two rights in Allah's wealth: a right for having seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a right for sharing with the Muslims; and he ordered that mounts be laden for him with grain and dates. So Abu Layla hastened, eating of the dates and grain, while Ibn al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Hardship has reached you, O Abu Layla. When he finished his craving he said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: As long as Quraysh rule justly, show mercy when mercy is sought, speak truthfully when they speak, and fulfill good promises when they promise, then I and the Prophets shall be at the Hawd as forerunners for the qasifin. He said: And al-qasifun are those who pour water upon the Hawd all at once. Ibn Abi Umar said: Wealth means camels."
"2513","Chapter of How the Pledge Is Given in Islam (3) (2107/1). Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I came to Madinah after Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had died and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) had been made caliph. I said to Umar: Raise your hand; I pledge to you upon what I pledged to your companion before you: hearing and obeying as much as I can."
"2514","(2107/2). And al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, similar to it. And at its end, meaning: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"2515","(2108). And al-Harith said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Burqan informed us, from Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj, from Abu al-Afif who said: I witnessed Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) pledging the people after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); groups would gather to him and he would say to them: Pledge to me upon hearing and obeying Allah and His Book, then the commander. I clung to my whip - I was then a youth near puberty or so - and when those with him left, I came to him and said: I pledge to you upon hearing and obeying Allah and His Book, and the commander. He said: So he raised his gaze at me and lowered it."
"2516","(2109). Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to me, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Asma bint Yazid who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the believing women to the pledge, and Asma said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not uncover your hand for us? He said: I do not shake hands with women. Its chain is good (hasan)."
"2517","(2110). Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard Asim al-Ahwal narrating from Amr ibn Atiyyah who said: I came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and pledged to him while a boy, upon the Book of Allah and the Sunnah of His Prophet - it is for us and against us - and he laughed and pledged me."
"2518","(2111). And Abu Ya'la said: Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Ghibtah Umm Amr, an old woman of Banu Mujashi', narrated to me, my paternal aunt narrated to me, from my grandmother, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Hind bint Utbah ibn Rabi'ah (may Allah be pleased with her) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pledge to him, and he said: Go and change your hand. She said: So she went and changed it with henna, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I pledge you upon that you associate nothing with Allah, steal not, and commit not adultery. She said: Does a free woman commit adultery? He said: And that you kill not your children for fear of poverty. She said: Have you left us any children to kill? She said: So she pledged to him. Then she said to him, wearing two gold bracelets: What do you say about these two bracelets? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two embers of the embers of Hell."
"2519","Chapter of the Religion Sometimes Being Supported by One with No Share in It (4) (2112). Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from al-Ifriqi, from Abd Allah ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, supports Islam with men who are not of its people."
"2520","(2113). And Musaddad said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi' who said: Amir ibn Abdah said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, surely supports this religion with the wicked man. Ibn Hibban authenticated it, but he brought it out through Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah."
"2521","Chapter of Preferring the Best Reciter for Leadership over the Nobler and Older (5) (2114). Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Tumaylah Yahya ibn Wadih narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment and tested their recitation; an old man recited, then a youth recited, and he appointed the youth. The old man said: You appointed him over me though I am older than him? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has more Qur'an than you."
"2522","(2115). And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: I heard my father saying: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to him, he said: I went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) to Makkah, and the governor of Makkah, Nafi' ibn Alqamah - calling himself by Nafi', an uncle of his - met us. He said: Whom did you leave over the people of Makkah? He said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza. He said: You went to a man of the freedmen and left him over those in it of Quraysh and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes; I found him the best reciter of them of the Book of Allah, and Makkah is a land much attended, so I wished they hear the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, from a man of good recitation. He said: Well have you judged; Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza is among those whom Allah raises by the Qur'an. Its men are trustworthy, but there is a question in it, for Abd al-Rahman is too young for that. And Muslim brought it out through al-Zuhri, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) with a different wording, in which is the story in meaning. And in it he said: Nafi' ibn al-Harith al-Khuza'i met him - and that is the preserved version."
"2523","Chapter of Standing over the Commander's Head with the Sword (6) (2116). Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Qays, from al-Mughirah: that he was standing over the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while Urwah ibn Mas'ud was speaking to him; al-Mughirah said to him: You shall surely withhold your hand, or it shall not return to you - and al-Mughirah was wearing a sword. Urwah said: Who is this? He said: This is your nephew al-Mughirah. He said: O treacherous one, I have not yet washed my head of your treachery. This hadith is sahih; al-Bukhari brought it out in the long hadith of the story of al-Hudaybiyah, from the narration of al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar."
"2524","Chapter of the Dislike of a Judge Ruling While Angry (7) (2117/1). Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from a man, from al-Hasan, that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed and said: By Allah, I am not the best of you; I was averse to this station of mine, and I wished one among you would suffice me. Do you think I shall act among you by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Then I could not uphold it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was protected by revelation, and with him was an angel; and I have a devil that besets me. When I am angry, keep away from me lest I affect your hair and skin. Beware, watch me: if I am straight, help me; if I deviate, straighten me. Al-Hasan said: By Allah, no sermon like it was ever delivered after him."
"2525","(2117/2). Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed, and he mentioned the like of it. Ahmad narrated part of it in meaning from the hadith of Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"2526","Chapter of the Commander's Retaliation from His Official for His Subjects (8) (2118/1). Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) would command his officials and they would meet him at the pilgrimage season, and he would say: O people, I did not employ your officials - or he said: my officials - to take of your skins, your wealth, or your honors; I only employed them over you to stand between you and to divide among you. Whoever has a grievance against any of them, let him stand. None of them stood that day except one man, who said: O Commander of the Faithful, your official struck me a hundred lashes. He said: Stand and retaliate from him. Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Commander of the Faithful, if you open this upon your officials it shall become a practice followed after you. He said: Shall I not retaliate from him, when I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) retaliating from himself? Amr said: Let us satisfy him. He said: So they satisfied him, ransoming from him with two hundred dinars, two dinars for every lash."
"2527","(2118/2). Jarir informed us, from Layth, from Ata, from Umar, similar to it. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Ahmad brought out its meaning in a hadith, but in it is not what is at its end."
"2528","(2119/1). And Musaddad said: Yazid narrated to us, Sa'id al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Firas who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us and said: O people, we knew you while among us was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: And I do not send my officials to strike your skins ... then he mentioned it; and Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) leapt up and said: O Commander of the Faithful, tell me: if a Muslim man were over subjects and disciplined one of his subjects, would you retaliate from him? He said: Yes, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Umar; how shall I not retaliate from him, when I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) retaliating from himself? Beware, do not strike the Muslims lest you humiliate them, nor withhold their rights lest you make them disbelieve, nor keep them long in expeditions lest you tempt them, nor settle them in thickets lest you waste them."
"2529","(2119/2). Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, Sa'id al-Jurayri narrated to me, in full."
"2530","Chapter Explaining the Saying of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): I Saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Retaliating from Himself (9) (2120). Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muhammad al-Muwaqqari narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Abu Hurayrah, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day urged jihad, and they crowded upon him until they pressed him; in his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a palm stalk whose thorns had been stripped except one thorn unnoticed. He said: Move back from me like this; you have pressed me. The Prophet struck the belly of a man and drew blood from the man; he went out saying: This is the deed of your Prophet - then what of the people? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) heard him and said to him: Go to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if he struck you, he will surely give you your right, and if you lied I shall frighten you with your treatment until you speak. The man said: Go in peace; I do not wish to go with you. He said: I shall not leave you. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) took him until he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: This one claims you struck him and bloodied his belly; what do you think? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it true I struck you? The man said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did anyone see that? He said: There were people of the Muslims here. He said: O Allah, I call upon the testimony of a man who saw that to inform me. Some Muslims said: O Messenger of Allah, you bloodied him though you did not intend him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take wealth for what I struck and go. He said: No. He said: Then grant it to me? He said: I shall not. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then what do you want? He said: I want to retaliate from you, O Prophet of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. The man said to him: Come out from among these; so he came out from among them and let the man have the palm stalk to retaliate from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He uncovered his belly, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to hold the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from behind, and he said: You stumbled with your sandal and your teeth broke. When the man drew near to strike the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he cast down the palm stalk, kissed his navel, and said: O Prophet of Allah, this is what I wanted, so that the tyrants after you may be suppressed. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You are surer in deed than I."
"2531","Chapter of the Commander Disciplining His Official When He Veils Himself from the Subjects or Exalts Himself over Them (10) (2121/1). Ishaq said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us, Abu Hayyan al-Tamimi narrated to us, from Abayah ibn Rafi' ibn Khadij who said: It reached Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sa'd had taken a door, then said: The noise is cut off. So he sent to Muhammad ibn Maslamah (may Allah be pleased with him); he came to him and said: Go to Sa'd and burn his door, then take his hand and bring him out to the people and say: Here, sit for the people. He said: So Muhammad sent his slave in his place to his house and ordered him to bring him two mounts and provisions from his family, and he walked toward Kufah until he came to the cemetery of Kufah. He saw a Nabatean entering Kufah with reeds upon a donkey, selling them; he bought them from him and stipulated that he drop them at the commander's door. He came until he dropped his reeds at the commander's door and struck his flint. Sa'd came, and it was said: Here is a black, tall, great man between a waist-wrapper and cloak, wearing a dark-red silk turban without a cap. He said: That is Muhammad ibn Maslamah; let him attain his need; let no man hinder him with anything. So he burned the door until it became charcoal. Then Sa'd came out to him; he asked him, and he swore by Allah he had not spoken the word that reached the Commander of the Faithful - a liar had conveyed it to him. He said: He offered him the house to enter, but he refused and turned back at once. He said: So Sa'd sent his provisions after him, and he returned them with his messenger, saying: Return your food to your companion, for he has dependents and we have surplus of our provisions. He said: So he traveled days without provisions; the first humans we met were a woman with sheep. Muhammad ibn Maslamah stood praying, and the slave went and bargained with the owner of the sheep for a small sheep of her flock for a headband he wore. He said: So he threw it down wanting to slaughter it, while Muhammad was standing praying, and he signaled to him not to slaughter it. When he finished he said: What is this sheep? If the flock has its owner, bargain with him or complete the slave-woman's sale and bring it; if she is only a shepherdess, return it, for hunger is better than evil food. He said: Then he traveled until he came to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him of what had happened and of what Sa'd had sent after him, which he had returned with his messenger. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What prevented you from accepting from him?"
"2532","(2121/2). And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan who said: I heard Abayah ibn Rifa'ah saying: It reached the Commander of the Faithful Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) had taken a door, then said: The voice is cut off. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sent to him and burned it; then Muhammad ibn Maslamah (may Allah be pleased with him) took his hand and brought him out, saying: Here, sit for the people. So Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) excused himself to him and swore to him: I did not speak the word that reached the Commander of the Faithful."
"2533","(2122). And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Harmalah ibn Imran, from Ka'b ibn Alqamah who said: Gharafah ibn al-Harith (may Allah be pleased with him) had companionship - then he mentioned the hadith. He said: And Gharafah said to Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him): When you sit with us you recline among us; do not do so, for if you repeat I shall write to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). So Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) repeated, and Gharafah wrote; and Umar's messenger came to Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: It has reached me that when you sit with your companions you recline among them as the non-Arabs do; do not do so. Sit with them as long as you sit, and when you enter your house do as you like. Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Gharafah (may Allah be pleased with him): You have established a case against me with Umar. Gharafah said: You never knew me a liar. He said: After that, Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) would want to recline, then remember and sit, saying: Allah is between me and Gharafah. He said: And they went out one day; it was a foggy day, and Gharafah's horse went ahead of Amr's horse. Amr said: My day with Gharafah is not a single one. It was said to Gharafah: The commander said such-and-such. He said: I did not see him because of the fog. It was said: Then apologize to him. He said: Do not accustom them to this. They kept at him until he came to him and said: I did not see you because of the fog. He said: O Allah, forgiveness; had you wished you could have held your horse. He said: By Allah, I wished he would cast you at the farthest stone in the meadow; I apologize to you with the fog, that I did not see you, and you say: O Allah, forgiveness? Amr said: O Abu al-Harith, I saw you with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on such-and-such a day upon a docile horse; shall we not mount you upon a horse? He said: I never knew you, O Amr, to provide horses; whence is this?"
"2534","Chapter of the Minister, and the Minister Rejecting the Commander's Order When He Sees Benefit in Opposing It (12) (2123). Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Dinar, from Ibn Sirin, from Abidah who said: Uyaynah ibn Hisn and al-Aqra' ibn Habis (may Allah be pleased with them both) came to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, we have a salty land in which there is no pasture nor benefit; if you see fit to grant it to us? So he granted it to them and wrote them a document concerning it, having Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) witness it - though he was not among the people. They went to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) to have him witness it; when Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) heard what was in the document, he took it from their hands, spat in it and erased it. They grumbled and said to him an evil saying. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to conciliate hearts when Islam was then few; Allah has honored Islam, so go and strive your utmost against me - may Allah not pasture you if you pasture."
"2535","Chapter of the Judge's Reward When He Strives for the Truth (13) (2124). Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ibrahim - a man of the family of Abu Rabi'ah - that it reached him that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), when he was made caliph, sat in his house grieving. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon him, turning to blame him, saying: You have burdened me with this matter; and he complained to him of judging between people. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the ruler strives and hits the truth, he has two rewards; and when he strives and misses the truth, he has one reward? It was as if the hadith of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) eased Abu Bakr."
"2536","(2125). Abu Ya'la said: Muhriz ibn Awn narrated to us, Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Ala, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him), from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Two disputants came disputing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Amr, judge between them. I said: O Prophet of Allah, you are more entitled to that. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even so. I said: Upon what shall I judge? He said: If you hit the judgment between them, you have ten good deeds; and if you strive and err, you have one good deed. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Faraj is weak; and the hadith is in the two Sahihs from Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) with a different wording, in which: When he strives and hits aright, he has two rewards."
"2537","(2126). And with it to Faraj, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Uqbah ibn Amir, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Amr, except that he said: rewards instead of good deeds."
"2538","Chapter of What Is Required of the Commander of Good Conduct and Not Veiling Himself (14) (95) There shall come, if Allah the Exalted wills, in the chapter of warning against innovations, the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Buraydah from Umar, in which is the admonition of al-Rabi' ibn Ziyad. (2127). And Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from al-Rabi' ibn Ziyad al-Harithi: that he came as a delegate to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and his appearance and manner pleased him; and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) complained of coarse food he had eaten. Al-Rabi' said: O Commander of the Faithful, the most entitled of people to soft food, soft clothing, and an easy mount is you. So he struck his head with a palm stalk and said: By Allah, by this you intended only to draw near me; I used to think good of you. Shall I not tell you my likeness and theirs? The likeness of a people who traveled and entrusted their expenses to a man among them, saying: Spend them upon us - does he have the right to prefer himself over them with anything? Al-Rabi' said: No. He said: This is my likeness and theirs. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I do not employ my officials to revile your honors ... the hadith."
"2539","(2128/1). And Musaddad said: Bishr - that is Ibn al-Mufaddal - narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Amr ibn Sa'id, from Abu Zur'ah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Hayyah bint Abi Hayyah who said: A man entered upon me at midday; I said: What is your need, O servant of Allah? He said: I came with a companion of mine seeking camels of ours; my companion went off seeking, and I entered the shade to rest and drink of the drink. She said: So I rose to a sour milk-skin of ours - sometimes she said: to a sour milk-bowl - and gave him to drink of it; and I studied him and said: O servant of Allah, who are you? He said: Abu Bakr. I said: Abu Bakr, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of whom I heard? He said: Yes. He said: So she mentioned our raiding Khath'am in the pre-Islamic period, and our raiding one another, and what Allah the Exalted brought of affection and the ropes of tents - thus he interlaced his fingers. I said: O servant of Allah, how long shall this affair of the people last? He said: As long as the imams are upright. She said: I said: And what are the imams? He said: Have you not seen the chief in the encampment whom they follow and obey? They are those, as long as they are upright."
"2540","(2128/2). And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Isma'il Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, with it."
"2541","Chapter of What Is Lawful for the Official of the Subjects' Wealth (15) (2129/1). Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman who said: Utbah ibn Farqad sent to Umar a khabis well prepared, made in baskets covered with felts. When it reached Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), he uncovered the dish of khabis, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do the Muslims in their camps eat their fill of this? The messenger said: No. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I do not want it; and he wrote to Utbah (may Allah be pleased with him): As for what follows: It is not of your earning nor of your mother's earning; so feed those Muslims before you in their camps of what you eat your fill of in your camp. The rest of it they brought out."
"2542","(2129/2). Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us with it."
"2543","(2129/3). And al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, from Abu Uthman who said: I was with Utbah ibn Farqad at Adharbayjan; he sent Suhaym and another to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) upon three mounts with two boxes, putting khabis in them, putting leather over them, and felts over the leather. When they arrived at Madinah, it was said: Suhaym the freedman of Utbah and another have come upon three mounts. He gave them leave and they entered; Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) asked them: Gold or silver? They said: No. He said: Then what have you brought? They said: Food. He said: Food of two men upon three mounts! Bring what you have brought. It was brought, and he uncovered the felts and leather. Umar came and put his hand in it and found it soft. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do all the Emigrants eat their fill of this? He said: No, but this is something singled out for the Commander of the Faithful. He said: O so-and-so, bring the inkpot. Write: From Abd Allah the Commander of the Faithful to Utbah ibn Farqad and those Muslims with him: Peace upon you; as for what follows: I praise to you Allah besides Whom there is no god. As for what follows: It is not of your earning nor your father's earning, nor your mother's earning, O Utbah ibn Farqad. Then he mentioned the hadith. He said: And in Umar's (may Allah be pleased with him) letter: Cut off riding in comfort, and leap upon horses leaping. Abu Uthman said: I saw the old man leap and fall upon his belly, and leap and fall upon his belly; then I saw him after that leaping as the boy leaps."
"2544","(2129/4). And Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim al-Sami narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, similar to it."
"2545","(2130/1). And al-Harith said: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The chief of the Copts gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) two slave-girls, sisters, and a mule. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would ride the mule at Madinah, took one of the slave-girls for himself and she bore him Ibrahim, and he gifted the other to Hassan ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"2546","(2130/2). Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us with it."
"2547","(2131). Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from al-Hasan who said: A man who mixed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the pre-Islamic period, called Iyad, gifted him a gift; he said: Have you embraced Islam? He said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The support of the polytheists is not lawful for us - meaning their aid. This is disconnected (mursal); and Iyad ibn Himar narrated the like of this; Abu Dawud and others brought it out with a sound chain."
"2548","(2132). And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Abu Hayyan narrated to us, Mujammi' narrated to me: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) would sweep the treasury, then two men would pray in it, bearing witness that he did not withhold wealth in it from the Muslims. (96) The hadith: The gifts of officials are all unlawful - in the chapter of the just Imam."
"2549","Chapter of the Hima (16) (2133). Al-Harith said: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father Ka'b ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to mark the hima of Madinah upon the heights of Dhat al-Jaysh, upon the landmarks of al-Masnu'ah, upon the heights of Makhid, and upon the heights of Qanah."
"2550","Chapter of Warning against Wrongdoing and Aiding Wrongdoers (17) (2134). Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us. Then he mentioned the hadith ... And in it: Whoever undertakes the dispute of a people with injustice, or aids them in it, the angel shall descend upon him bringing tidings of curse and Fire, abiding therein - an evil destination. Whoever hastens to an unjust ruler for a need is his companion in the Fire. Whoever guides a ruler to injustice shall be joined with Haman in the Fire, and he and that ruler shall be among the most severely punished of people. Whoever slaps the cheek of a Muslim, Allah the Exalted shall scatter his bones on the Day of Resurrection, then subject him to the Fire, and he shall be raised, when raised, bound until he enters the Fire. Whoever hangs a whip before an unjust ruler, Allah the Exalted shall make for him a snake seventy thousand cubits long to be set upon him in the Fire of Hell, abiding forever. Whoever informs against his brother to the ruler, Allah shall nullify all his deeds; if harm or injury reaches him, Allah the Exalted shall place him with Haman in his rank in the Fire. Whoever takes charge of the headmanship of a people shall be held upon the brink of Hell for a thousand years for every day, and raised with his hand bound to his neck; if he established Allah's command among them he shall be released, and if he was unjust he shall fall into the Fire of Hell for seventy autumns. And in it: Verily Allah the Exalted - glorified be His praise - does not wrong, nor does wrong befit Him; He is ever watchful: That He may recompense those who do evil for what they did, and recompense those who do good with good. Whoever does good, it is for himself; whoever does evil, it is against him; and your Lord is not unjust to the servants."
"2551","(2135). And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, Husayn ibn Qays al-Rahabi narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no one stand by a man being killed wrongfully, for the curse descends upon whoever is present when they did not defend him; and let no one stand by a man being beaten wrongfully, for the curse descends upon whoever is present when they did not defend him."
"2552","Chapter of Patience upon the Commander's Discipline (18) (2136). Ishaq said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: I heard my father saying: Abu Nadrah informed us, from Abu Sa'id the freedman of Abu Usayd - that is Malik ibn Rabi'ah - who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) forbade Umrah in the months of Hajj, or forbade enjoyment of Umrah with Hajj; so Ali performed it in his place. Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) came down from the pulpit, took something and walked with it to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) - Talhah and al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with them both) stood and snatched it from him - and he walked to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), almost poking his eye with his finger, saying to him: You are indeed misguided and misguiding; and Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) returned nothing to him."
"2553","(2137). Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, he said: Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, from Imran ibn Abd Allah ibn Talhah al-Khuza'i, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab who said: I witnessed Ali and Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both); there was a prompting of Satan between them, and neither left anything unsaid to his companion. Had I wished to narrate to you what was between them I would have done so; then they did not move until each of them sought forgiveness for his companion."
"2554","(2138). And Abu Bakr said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Talhah ibn Amr narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from Abu al-Juwayriyah al-Jarmi, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Jarmi who said: I was sitting with Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) when an old man came; when the people saw him they said: Abu Dharr. When Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) saw him he said: Welcome and greetings to my brother - Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Welcome and greetings to my brother - By my life, you have been stern in the resolve; by Allah, had you resolved that I crawl, I would have crawled as much as I could crawl."
"2555","(2139). Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Abd Allah who said: A man came and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what do you say about a man keen upon jihad and dutiful, whose commanders resolve upon him things beyond count? He said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to you except that we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); perhaps we would be commanded nothing but we did it."
"2556","Chapter of Encouraging Obedience, and that the Religion May Be Supported by the Wicked (19) (2140). Abu Bakr said: Aswad ibn Amir and Ali ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sharik, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever dies with no obedience upon him dies the death of pre-Islamic ignorance; and whoever casts it off after contracting it - Aswad said: from his neck - shall meet Allah the Exalted with no proof for him. Both said it."
"2557","(2141). Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to me, from his father, from al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We used to recite: Allah the Exalted shall surely support this religion with men who have no share in the Hereafter."
"2558","(2142). Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Awwam, from Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Prayer to prayer is expiation for what is between them, except for three: associating with Allah, abandoning the Sunnah, and breaking the bargain. They said: We know associating with Allah; what are abandoning the Sunnah and breaking the bargain? He said: Abandoning the Sunnah is leaving obedience, and breaking the bargain is that a man pledges to a man then rises against him with the sword to fight him."
"2559","(2143/1). And Abd said: Umar ibn Sa'id al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Tanukhi narrated to us, from Makhul, from Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counseling some of his household, he said: And do not dispute command with its people, even if you see it is the truth."
"2560","(2143/2). And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, Abu Mushir narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us with it. (2143/3). And Abd said: Umar narrated to us, others than Sa'id narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, that the one counseled with this counsel was Thawban."
"2561","(2144). And Ishaq said: Baqiyyah informed us, Artah ibn al-Mundhir narrated to me, from one who narrated to him, from Umm Salamah that she said to those with her: How will you be when there are two callers: a caller to the Book of Allah, and a caller to the authority of Allah? They said: We shall answer the caller to the Book of Allah. She said: Rather answer the caller to the authority of Allah, for the Book of Allah is with His authority."
"2562","(2145). Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq narrated to us, al-Nadr narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak who said: I heard Alqamah ibn Wa'il saying: Salamah ibn Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Tell me, if there stand over us rulers who ask us their right and withhold our right? He fell silent two or three times. He narrated it to al-Ash'ath ibn Qays, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hear and obey, for upon them is what they were burdened with, and upon you is what you were burdened with."
"2563","(2146). And Musaddad said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Khalid, from Shaqiq who said: Abd Allah said: You have been tested with this authority, and it has been tested with you; if it is just, it has the reward and upon you is gratitude, and if it is unjust, upon it is the burden and upon you is patience. Sahih, halted (mawquf)."
"2564","(2147). And Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Thabit ibn al-Ajlan narrated to us, one who heard the like of it narrated to me - and he was among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that Umm Salamah wrote to the people of Iraq: Allah is free, and His Messenger is free, of whoever partisans and splits the community; so do not partisan nor split. Peace upon you and the mercy of Allah."
"2565","Chapter of Appointing as Official One Skilled in War over One Better Than Him (20) (2148). Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, from al-Mundhir ibn Tha'labah, from Ibn Buraydah who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) when Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) prevented the people from kindling fire: Do you not see what this one does with the people, withholding their benefits? Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Leave him, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only appointed him over us for his knowledge of war. This is disconnected (munqati')."
"2566","(2149). Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed after the expedition of Dhat al-Salasil Usamah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) while a boy. In that expedition many Arabs were captured and taken captive; Umar ibn al-Khattab and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with them both) volunteered in the expedition of Usamah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died before that army set out, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dispatched it after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Usamah said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) when allegiance was pledged to him - and Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) did not depart until allegiance was pledged to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) - he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directed me to what he directed me to, and I fear the Arabs may apostatize; if you wish I shall stay near until you see. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I shall not turn back a matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded; but if you wish to permit Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), do so. So he permitted him. Usamah (may Allah be pleased with him) set out until he came to the place the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded him; fog seized them until a man could hardly see his companion. He said: They found a man of those lands, seized him, and he guided them to the road where they aimed; they raided the place they were commanded. The people heard of that, and some said to others: Do they claim the Arabs have differed, while their horses are at such-and-such a place? So Allah the Exalted thereby warded off from the Muslims. Usamah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) was called by the title of command until he died; they would say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him forth and did not remove him until he died. Al-Zuhri said: When Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent to fight the apostates, he said: Proceed deliberately; whatever quarter you hear the adhan in, hold back, for the adhan is the emblem of faith. Ma'mar said: And Hisham ibn Urwah said: The apostates would come to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: Give us weapons to fight them with, and he would give them weapons and they would fight him. Abbas ibn Mirdas al-Sulami (may Allah be pleased with him) said:"
"2567","Chapter of the Merit of the Just Imam and Censure of the Unjust (21) (2150). Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir ibn Qunfudh, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The best of Allah's servants, Mighty and Majestic, in station with Allah the Exalted on the Day of Resurrection is a just, gentle imam; and the worst of Allah's servants, Exalted, in station with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, on the Day of Resurrection is an unjust, harsh imam."
"2568","(2151). Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Mubashshir ibn Ubayd al-Himsi narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Masruq, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever is put in charge of anything of the Muslims' affair and is not just among them, upon him is the bahlah of Allah. And the bahlah of Allah is the curse of Allah."
"2569","(2252). And al-Harith said: Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Mikhraq, from a man, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The justice of the official among his subjects for a single day is better than the worship of the worshipper among his family for a hundred years - or fifty years; Hushaym is the doubter."
"2570","(2253). And Abu Ya'la said: Khalid ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Bakr narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Salim, from Amr ibn Dirar, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I am not one to praise a governor. It was said: Why is that? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The rulers shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection, their just and their unjust, until they stand upon the bridge of Hell; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, shall say: In you is My demand. No unjust one in his judgment, taking bribes in his judging, lending his ear to one of the two disputants, shall remain but he shall fall into the Fire for seventy autumns."
"2571","He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The gifts of officials are all unlawful."
"2572","He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who employs a man over ten souls, knowing among the ten one better than the one he employed, has deceived Allah and His Messenger, and deceived the community of the Muslims. And the one who struck beyond the limit shall be brought and He shall say: My servant, why did you strike beyond what I commanded you? He shall say: I was angry. He shall say: Was your anger to be more severe than My anger? And the one who fell short shall be brought and He shall say: My servant, why did you fall short? He shall say: I had mercy on him. He shall say: Was your mercy to be more severe than My mercy? And both shall be ordered to the Fire."
"2573","(2256). And Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Qays, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever employs a man over a group, while among that group is one more pleasing to Allah than him, has betrayed Allah and betrayed His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and betrayed all the believers. Al-Hakim brought it out through Ibn Lahi'ah, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ikrimah."
"2574","(2257). And Musaddad said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Mu'alla, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two kinds of my Ummah my intercession shall not reach - and my intercession shall never reach them, or I shall never intercede for them: an unjust, treacherous, oppressive commander, and every defrauding renegade."
"2575","(2258/1). And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Jamil narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Mani' narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah narrated to him, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two men of my Ummah my intercession shall not reach: an unjust, oppressive, treacherous imam, and another extreme in religion, renegade in it."
"2576","(2258/2). Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, al-Aghlab ibn Tamim narrated to us, al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the like of it, except he did not say oppressive."
"2577","(2259). And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Himsi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad al-Maliki, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No governor who trades among his subjects has been just."
"2578","(2260). And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Isma'il narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Ibn Umar, raising it, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted puts no servant in charge of subjects, few or many, but He shall ask him about them - whether his rule among them stood firm or was wasted - until He asks him even about his household in particular."
"2579","Chapter of Prohibiting Obedience in Other Than Good (22) (2261). Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir narrated to us, from al-A'sha ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mukmil, from Azhar ibn Abd Allah who said: Ubadah came on Hajj from Sham and arrived at Madinah; he came to Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Shall I not inform you of something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There shall be rulers over you who command you with what you recognize, and do what you deny; those have no obedience upon them."
"2580","(2262). And Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq narrated to us, Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Harb narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, Amr ibn Zunayb narrated to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him that Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me: if there are rulers over us who do not follow your Sunnah nor take your command, what do you command us concerning them? He said: There is no obedience to one who has not obeyed Allah."
"2581","Chapter of the Arifah (23) (2263/1). Abu Dawud said: Hisham narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Abi Ali, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The arifah - its beginning is blame, its end is regret, and punishment on the Day of Resurrection. I said: O Abu Hurayrah, except one among them who fears Allah. He said: I only narrate to you as I heard."
"2582","(2263/2). And Abu Ya'la said: Shuja' ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated to us with it."
"2583","(2164). And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu al-Ashhab, from Dabi' ibn Bashshar, from his uncle Sa'sa'ah ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: For the arif a door of Hell - or of the Fire - is opened every year."
"2584","(2165). And Musaddad said: Isma'il narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Ghilaq: I said to Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said: Do not be an arif nor a policeman. I said: Why? He said: They make you indebted and do not forget you."
"2585","(2166). Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard Shabib saying: Muqatil ibn Hayyan narrated to me, from a man of Banu Tamim, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not be an arif nor a policeman."
"2586","(2167). And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Salih ibn Salih - better than his two sons - narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi who said: They said: The man knows us over us. He said: Your arif is but the sly, clever, grey wolf, the toiling licker who when told Ha stops, and when told Bring shrinks back."
"2587","(2168). And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad narrated to us, Mubarak - that is Ibn Suhaym - narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A funeral passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Glad tidings for him, if he was not an arif."
"2588","(2169). Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, Ubays ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People must have an arif, and the arif is in the Fire. He said: And the policeman shall be brought and said to: Lay down your whip and enter the Fire."
"2589","(2170). Ishaq narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Raqabah ibn Masqalah, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mas'ud, from Abu Sa'id and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them both), both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall surely come upon people a time when there shall be over you foolish rulers, who advance the worst of people and delay their best, and delay prayer from its times. Whoever among you reaches that, let him not be an arif, nor a policeman, nor a collector, nor a traitor."
"2590","Chapter of the Commander's Covenant to His Officials, How They Should Conduct Themselves among Muslims and Disbelievers (24) (2171). Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, my father al-Muhabbar ibn Qahdham narrated to us, from al-Miswar ibn Abd Allah al-Bahili, from some of the children of al-Jarud, from al-Jarud, that he took this copy from the copy of the covenant of al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for him when he sent him to Bahrayn: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Kind. This is a writing from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah, the unlettered, Qurashi, Hashimi Prophet, the Messenger of Allah and His Prophet, to His creation all; for al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami and those Muslims with him - a covenant he covenants to you: Fear Allah, O Muslims, as much as you can, for I have sent over you al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami and appointed him, charging him to fear Allah alone with no partner, to be gentle to you, to conduct himself among you well with the truth, and to judge between you and whomever he meets of people by what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down in His Book of justice. And I command you to obey him when he does that, divides justly, and shows mercy when mercy is sought; so hear him and obey, and aid and assist him well. For I have upon you a right of obedience, a great right whose full measure you cannot grasp, nor can speech reach the essence of the greatness of Allah and the right of His Messenger - just as Allah and His Messenger have over people generally, and over you specially, a due obligatory right; obedience to Him and fulfillment of His covenant - and may Allah be pleased with whoever holds to obedience and honors the right of its people and the right of its rulers - so likewise the Muslims have over their rulers a due obligatory right and obedience. For in obedience is attainment of every good sought and safety from every evil feared. And I call Allah to witness over whomever I appoint over anything of the Muslims' affairs, little or much, and he is not just among them, that he has no obedience, he is stripped of what I appointed him to, and the pledge, oaths and covenant of the Muslims with him are absolved. At that, let them seek Allah's guidance, then employ over themselves the best of them. Verily, if misfortune befalls al-Ala, Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him), the sword of Allah among them, succeeds al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami; so hear him and obey as long as you know him upon the truth, until he departs from truth to other than it. Proceed upon Allah's blessing, aid, victory, well-being, guidance and success. Whomever you meet of people, invite them to the revealed Book of Allah and His practices and the practice of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to deem lawful what Allah made lawful for them in His Book, to deem unlawful what Allah made unlawful for them in His Book, to cast off rivals, disavow polytheism and disbelief, disbelieve in the worship of the Taghut, al-Lat and al-Uzza, to abandon the worship of Isa son of Maryam, Uzayr ibn Harwah, the angels, the sun, the moon, and everything taken as a rival besides Allah the Exalted, to take Allah and His Messenger as allies, and to disavow what Allah and His Messenger disavow. When they do that, affirm it and enter into allegiance, then explain to them at that what is in the Book of Allah to which you invite them; for it is the Book of Allah sent down with the Trustworthy Spirit upon His chosen one of the worlds, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah, His servant, Messenger, Prophet and beloved, whom He sent as a mercy to the worlds generally - the white among them and the black, men and jinn. The Book of Allah - in it is the tidings of everything before you and what shall be after you, to be a barrier between people by which Allah the Exalted holds back some from others. It is the Book of Allah, dominant over the Books, confirming what is in them of the Torah, the Gospel and the Psalms; Allah informs you in it of what was before you that escaped your grasp among your first forefathers to whom Allah's messengers and prophets came - how their answer was, with what He sent them, how they believed Allah's signs or denied them. And Allah the Exalted informed in His Book of their affair and deed, and the deed of those among them who perished in their religion, that you avoid that and not do the like, lest there befall you in Allah's Book of His punishment, wrath and vengeance like what befell them for their evil deeds. He made clear to you in this Book all of that affair, out of mercy from Him to you and compassion of your Lord upon you. It is guidance from error, clarity from blindness, recovery from stumbling, safety from trial, light from darkness, healing at events, protection from ruin, right guidance from deviation, clarity from confusion, and the separator between this world and the Hereafter; in it is the perfection of your religion. When you present this to them and they affirm it to you, complete the allegiance; then present to them at that Islam: the five prayers, giving zakat, Hajj to the House, fasting Ramadan, bathing from major impurity, purification before prayer, kindness to parents, joining the Muslim womb, and good companionship even to the two polytheist parents. When they do that they have embraced Islam. Then invite them after that to faith, and set out for them its laws and landmarks: faith by testifying there is no god but Allah alone with no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, that what Muhammad brought is the truth and what is besides it is falsehood; faith in Allah, His angels, His Books, His Messengers and Prophets, the Last Day; faith in what is before him and behind him of the Torah, the Gospel, the Psalms, resurrection, gathering, reckoning, Paradise, Fire, death and life; and faith in Allah and His Messenger, and for the believers all. When they do that and affirm it, they are submitting believers. Then guide them after that to excellence (ihsan), and teach them that excellence is to do well between them and Allah the Exalted in fulfilling the trust, the covenant He covenanted to His messengers, the covenant of His messengers to His creation and the imams of the believers, submission, the safety of the Muslims from every harm of tongue or hand, seeking for the rest of the Muslims as one seeks for oneself, belief in the promises of the Lord, meeting Him, watching Him, bidding farewell to this world every hour, reckoning the soul at the close of every day and night, provisioning from night and day, and attending to what Allah made obligatory to render to Him in secret and openly. When they do that they are submitting, believing, excellent ones. Then describe to them the major sins, guide them to them, and frighten them of ruin in major sins; for major sins are the destroyers, the first of them: associating with Allah - Allah does not forgive that He be associated with - sorcery, and the sorcerer has no share; severing kinship - Allah has cursed them; fleeing from battle - he has incurred wrath from Allah; ghulul - he shall bring what he misappropriated on the Day of Resurrection, and it shall not be accepted from them; killing the believing soul - his recompense is Hell; slandering the chaste - cursed in this world and the Hereafter; devouring the orphan's wealth - they eat fire in their bellies and shall roast in a blaze; devouring usury - then take notice of war from Allah and His Messenger. When they refrain from major sins they are submitting, believing, excellent, God-fearing ones. Then invite them likewise to worship: worship is fasting, standing, humility, submission, bowing, prostration, turning, certainty, devotion, declaring there is no god but Allah, glorification, praise, magnification, charity beyond zakat, humbleness, calm, consolation, supplication, imploring, affirming sovereignty and servitude, and deeming abundant righteous deeds little. When they do that they are believing, submitting, excellent, God-fearing, worshipping ones, having completed worship. Then invite them at that to jihad and explain it to them, urging them to what Allah urged them to of the merit of jihad and its reward with Allah. If they volunteer, pledge them and invite them until you pledge them to the practice of Allah and the practice of His Messenger: upon you is Allah's covenant and protection, seven guarantees - meaning Allah is guarantor over fulfillment seven times - that you shall not withdraw your hands from a pledge nor break the command of any governor of the Muslims. When they affirm this, pledge them and seek Allah's forgiveness for them. When they go forth fighting in Allah's path in anger for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in support of His religion, whomever they meet of people let them invite them to what they were invited to of Allah's Book - responding to it, then its Islam, faith, excellence, God-fearingness, worship and jihad. Whoever follows them is the urged, the abounding, submitting, believing, excellent, God-fearing, worshipping, striving one; he has what you have and upon him is what is upon you. Whoever refuses this against you, fight them until they return to Allah's command and His religion. With whomever you covenant and give Allah's protection, fulfill it to him. Whoever embraces Islam and gives you contentment is of you and you are of him. Whoever fights you over this after you have made him hear it, kill him. Whoever attacks you, fight him; whoever schemes against you, scheme against him; whoever gathers against you, gather against him; whoever deceives you, deceive him - or tricks you, trick him - without transgressing, or plots against you, plot against him without transgressing, secretly or openly. For whoever defends himself after being wronged, those have no way against them. Know Allah is with you, seeing you and your deeds, knowing all you do; so fear Allah and beware. This is but a trust my Lord entrusted me with, to convey it to His servants - an excuse from Him to them and a proof from Him by which I argue against whomever this writing reaches of all creation. Whoever acts by what is in it is saved; whoever follows by what is in it is guided; whoever disputes by it prospers; whoever fights by it is aided; whoever abandons it strays until he returns to it. So learn what is in it, lend your ears, contain it in your insides, commit it to your hearts; for it is the light of sights, the spring of hearts, healing for what is in breasts. Sufficient is this as a matter, a lesson, a deterrent, an admonition, a caller to Allah and His Messenger. This is the good in which there is no evil: the writing of Muhammad, Allah's servant, Messenger and Prophet, for al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami when he sent him to Bahrayn to invite to Allah and His Messenger, commanding him to invite to what is in it of lawful, forbid what is in it of unlawful, point to what is in it of guidance, and forbid what is in it of error. A writing over which Allah's Prophet entrusted al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami and his successor, Allah's sword Khalid ibn al-Walid; he has excused himself to them in the counsel with what is in this writing, and to those Muslims with them; he made no excuse for any of them in neglecting anything of it - neither the rulers nor the ruled. Whoever of all creation this writing reaches has no excuse nor proof, nor is he excused by ignorance of anything in this writing. This writing was written on the third of Dhu al-Qa'dah, four years having passed of the emigration of Allah's Prophet except two months. The writing witnessed, the day it was written - Ibn Abi Sufyan, with Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) dictating to him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting - al-Mukhtar ibn Qays al-Qurashi, Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman al-Absi, Qusayy ibn Abi Umar al-Himyari, Shabib ibn Abi Marthad al-Ghassani, al-Mustanir ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah al-Khuza'i, Awanah ibn Shamakh al-Juhani, Sa'd ibn Malik al-Ansari, Sa'd ibn Ubadah al-Ansari, Zayd ibn Umayr, the chiefs, a man of Quraysh, a man of Juhaynah, and four of the Ansar - until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed it to al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami and Allah's sword Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"2591","Judgments and Testimonies (22) (97) Much of it has preceded in the Book of the Caliphate and Leadership. Chapter of What Is Feared for One Who Judges without Right (1) (2172/1). Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Safwan ibn Amr al-Saksaki narrated to me, Shurayh ibn Ubayd and Murih ibn Masruq narrated to me, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The judge shall descend in his station in Hell farther than Adan."
"2592","(2172/2). And Abd said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, but he said: from Shurayh ibn Ubayd and Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, and he did not mention Murih ibn Masruq."
"2593","(2172/3). And thus Abu Ya'la brought it out from Suwayd ibn Sa'id, from Baqiyyah with it."
"2594","(2173/1). And Abd said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abu Sinan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Mawhab who said: Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): Judge between people. He said: I shall not judge between two men, nor lead them. He said: Your father (may Allah be pleased with him) used to judge. He said: My father used to judge; if anything was unclear to him he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if it was unclear to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he asked Jibril (peace be upon him); and I find none to ask, and I am not like my father. And it reached me that judges are three: a man who feared but desire swayed him, so he is in the Fire; a man who undertook judging and judged in ignorance, so he is in the Fire; and a man who strove and hit aright, so he escapes barely, neither for him nor against him. And did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever seeks refuge with Allah has sought refuge with a strong refuge? He said: Yes. He said: I seek refuge with Allah from you, that you make me a judge. So he excused him, saying: Tell no one."
"2595","(2173/2). And Abu Ya'la said: Shayban and Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us - separating them - and this is the wording of Umayyah; both said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Jamilah narrating from Abd Allah ibn Mawhab, who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): Go and be a judge. He said: Will the Commander of the Faithful excuse me? He said: Go and judge between people. He said: Will you excuse me, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: I insist upon you to go and judge. He said: Do not hasten; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever seeks refuge with Allah has sought refuge with a strong refuge. He said: Yes. He said: I seek refuge with Allah from being a judge. He said: And what prevents you, when your father was a judge? He said: Because I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever is a judge judging unjustly is of the people of the Fire; whoever is a judge judging in ignorance is of the people of the Fire; and whoever is a knowing judge and judges with truth or justice shall ask Allah to slip away barely. I (Ibn Hajar) say: al-Tirmidhi brought out part of it, saying: In it there is a break."
"2596","(2174). And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Nadr ibn Isma'il narrated to us, some of the elders narrated to us, from Nufay' Abu Dawud, from Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted is with the judge so long as he does not deliberately commit injustice, or so long as he does not swerve deliberately; and He guides him to the truth so long as he intends nothing else."
"2597","(2175). And Musaddad said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, Fitr narrated to us, from al-Dhayyal ibn Harmalah, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is put in charge of people and veils himself from them at their poverty and need, Allah shall veil Himself from him on the Day of Resurrection. I (Ibn Hajar) say: Abu Dawud narrated it through Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Maryam al-Azdi, that he said to Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying ... then he mentioned this hadith. Al-Tirmidhi said: This Abu Maryam al-Azdi, his name is Amr ibn Murrah."
"2598","(2176). And Abu Ya'la said: Khalid ibn Mirdas narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Kathir, from Abu Abd Allah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you is tried with judging between Muslims, let him not judge while angry; let him equalize between them in seating, looking and gesturing; and let him not raise his voice over one disputant above the other."
"2599","(2177). And Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Hariz, from Shurayh: that when he was angry or hungry he would stand and judge between no one."
"2600","(2178). And al-Harith said: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Abd Allah al-Umari narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no judge judge except while full and quenched."
"2601","(2179). And Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Judging is not a calculation you calculate; it is but a wiping that passes over the heart."
"2602","(2180). And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid who said: When Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked about something, if it was in the Book of Allah the Exalted he spoke by it. If it was not in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he informed of it. If it was not in the Book of Allah nor in the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and was from Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he took by it. If it was not from them, he strove with his opinion."
"2603","Chapter of Forbidding Honoring One Disputant, and Disputing without Right (2) (2181). Ishaq said: Muhammad ibn al-Fadl informed us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan who said: A man came and stayed with Ali (may Allah be pleased with him); he hosted him, and he said: I want to dispute. Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Move on, for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to host a disputant except with his opponent present."
"2604","(2182). And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Nu'aym narrated to us, from his father, who said: I witnessed Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) judging; al-Harith ibn al-Hakam came and sat upon his cushion on which he reclined. He said: Abu Hurayrah thought he had come for a need other than judgment. He said: Then a man came and sat before Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him); he said to him: What is your case? He said: Al-Harith has claimed against me. Abu Hurayrah said to him: Stand and sit with your opponent, for it is the practice of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2605","(2183). And Abu Bakr said: Shabbabah narrated to us, Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Muttalib who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever presses upon - heedlessly - a disputant without knowledge of his dispute shall remain in Allah's wrath until he desists."
"2606","Chapter of the Dislike of Payment for Judgment (3) (2184). Musaddad said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Musa ibn Tarif, from his father, who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) divided a division and called a man to count among people; they said: Give him. He said: If he wishes - and it is ill-gotten."
"2607","Chapter of Censuring Bribery (4) (2185). Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ma'n al-Mujashi'i, from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Khalaf al-Talhi, from a man of the Emigrants who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The briber and the bribed are in the Fire."
"2608","(2186/1). He said: And Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Yahya, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Amrah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the briber and the bribed."
"2609","(2186/2). Abu Ya'la narrated it from Ahmad ibn Mani'. And al-Bazzar said: We know it from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) only with this chain; Ishaq ibn Yahya is alone in it, and he is lenient (layyin)."
"2610","(2187/1). And Musaddad said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Fitr, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Salim, from Masruq who said: A man asked Abd Allah about ill-gotten gain? He said: Bribes. He said: And injustice in judgment? He said: That is disbelief."
"2611","2187/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Fitr narrated to us with this [chain], and his wording: I was sitting with Abdullah, and a man said to him: What is suht (illicit gain)? He said: Bribes. He said: And injustice in judgment? He said: That is disbelief. Then he recited: {And whoever does not judge by what Allah has revealed, then those are the disbelievers}. Shu'bah corroborated him, from Mansur; al-Hakim transmitted it."
"2612","5 - Chapter on judging with proof, and the oath with the witness. 2188 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, Hajjaj [narrated to us], from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with them: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Proof is upon the claimant, and the oath is upon the one against whom the claim is made."
"2613","2189/1 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us. 2189/2 - And Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us - both said: Hajjaj ibn Abi Uthman al-Sawwaf narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he ruled: when the seeker [claimant] has no proof, then the oath is upon the one against whom the claim is made - the wording is Ishaq's. And the other said: Whoever seeks a claim without witnesses, then the one against whom it is sought has more right to the oath."
"2614","2189/3 - And Ishaq said: And Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, with this chain, the like of it."
"2615","2190 - Abu Ya'la said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin: He said: Shurayh used to say: [Bring] two just witnesses [to testify] that you two parted by mutual consent after the sale, or by [exercise of] the option; otherwise his oath by Allah that you two did not part by mutual consent after the sale, or by option. Its chain is sahih, halted [mawquf] upon Shurayh."
"2616","6 - Chapter on the oath with the witness. 2191/1 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he ruled with the oath along with the [single] witness. My father said: And I bear witness that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, ruled with it amongst you. Abd al-Aziz said: Muhammad ibn Ali says it to al-Hakam ibn Utaybah."
"2617","2191/2 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad: He said: I heard my father saying it to al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, and he mentioned it. And al-Tirmidhi transmitted it from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir, and pointed to the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2618","2192 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, Abu al-Zinad narrated to me: He said: I was with Abd al-Hamid at Kufa, and he used to rule with the oath along with the witness, so some people of Kufa objected to him for that, so he wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, may Allah be pleased with him, so he wrote [ordering] to rule with the oath along with the witness. Then an old man from their elders stood and said: I witnessed Shurayh ruling with the oath along with the witness in this mosque."
"2619","7 - Chapter on whose testimony is not accepted and is rejected. 2193 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The kubah [a drum] is unlawful, and the duff [tambourine] is unlawful, and string instruments are unlawful, and wind instruments are unlawful."
"2620","2194 - Abu Dawud said: Shu'bah narrated to us, a man from the family of Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ammar, from Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A dayyuth [shameless one who condones indecency] will not enter Paradise."
"2621","2195 - Musaddad said: Hafs narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd al-Qurashi, from Talhah ibn Abdillah ibn Awf: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a caller, so he called out when he reached the mountain pass, and he said: The testimony of an opponent [in the dispute] and of one suspect is not valid, and the oath is upon the one against whom the claim is made. Abu Dawud transmitted it in al-Marasil."
"2622","2196 - Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar: He said: Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah wrote to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, asking him about the testimony of children, so he said: It is not valid, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: {from among those whom you approve as witnesses}, [and about Zanjs who slaughtered a donkey: that their master guarantees it, so there is no amputation upon them]."
"2623","2197 - Isma'il narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The testimony of a child against a child, and the testimony of a slave against a slave, is valid."
"2624","2198 - Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us - from Umar ibn Rashid - from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One religion does not inherit from another religion, and the testimony of one religion against another religion is not valid, except the community of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for their testimony is valid against others than them."
"2625","2199 - Mu'tamir narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, from Ma'mar, from Musa ibn Shaybah: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rejected the testimony of a man due to his lying."
"2626","2200/1 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah: He said: Beware of these poisonous dice that are cast with a throw, for they are but from gambling."
"2627","2200/2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, the like of it."
"2628","2201 - Abu Ya'la said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar ibn Maysarah narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from al-Ju'ayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman - he is al-Khatmi - that he heard Muhammad ibn Ka'b - al-Qurazi - asking Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id - al-Khudri - may Allah be pleased with him: What did you hear your father narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I heard my father saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The example of one who plays with dice, then stands and prays, is like one who performs ablution with pus and the blood of a pig; he says: His prayer is not accepted."
"2629","8 - Chapter on the dislike of bearing testimony in what is disliked to do. 2202 - Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a son of his, and said [to him]: O Messenger of Allah, I want you to witness a charity I give to this son of mine. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you have another son besides him? He said: Yes. He said: So did you give him the like of this? He said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then I will not bear witness."
"2630","9 - Chapter on deterring from false testimony and from concealing true testimony. 2203 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them: Both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever retracts a testimony or conceals it, Allah will feed him his own flesh before all creatures, then admit him into the Fire while he chews his tongue; and whoever bears false witness against a Muslim or a disbeliever will be hung by his tongue on the Day of Resurrection, then placed with the hypocrites in the lowest depth of the Fire; and whoever claims to have seen [in a dream] what he did not see is like one who bore false witness - the hadith. This hadith is fabricated."
"2631","2204/1 - al-Harith [also] said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father, from Hanash, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: Whoever bears a testimony by which he makes lawful the wealth of a Muslim man, or sheds his blood through it, has made the Fire obligatory for himself."
"2632","2204/2 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated to us with this."
"2633","And Ubaydullah ibn Umar - he is al-Qawariri - narrated to us, from Mu'tamir, the like of it."
"2634","2205 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Asim ibn Ubaydillah narrated to me, [from Abdullah ibn Amir] ibn Rabi'ah: He said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, detain a false witness [tied] in a lower garment in the evening, reproaching himself, then he let him go. And the hadith of Muharib ibn Dithar, may Allah be pleased with him, will come, Allah willing, in al-Mawa'iz."
"2635","23 - Book of clothing and adornment. 1 - Chapter on cursing the woman who joins hair and the one who has it joined. 2206 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, al-Qasim and Makhul narrated to us, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed - meaning on the day of Khaybar - the woman who joins hair and the one whose hair is joined, and the woman who tattoos and the one who is tattooed."
"2636","2 - Chapter on the command to clean houses. 2207 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Ilyas, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Exalted, is Good and loves what is good, Clean and loves cleanliness, Generous and loves generosity, Munificent and loves munificence; so clean your courtyards, and do not resemble the Jews who gather rubbish in their houses. In it is Khalid; weak."
"2637","3 - Chapter on encouragement to [wear] the turban. 2208/1 - al-Tayalisi said: al-Ash'ath ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Busr narrated to us, from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turbaned me on the day of Ghadir Khumm with a turban whose end he let hang behind me, then he said: Allah, Exalted, supported me on the day of Badr and Hunayn with angels wearing this turban, and he said: The turban is a barrier between disbelief and faith - the hadith."
"2638","2208/2 - Abu Bakr and Ahmad ibn Mani' both said: Ali ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sa'id, Abdullah narrated to us, from Abu Rashid: He said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: He turbaned me - and he mentioned it - except that he said: So he let its two ends hang upon my shoulders, and said: A barrier between the Muslims and the polytheists."
"2639","4 - Chapter on deterring from wearing a garment and trailing it out of pride, except from necessity. 2209 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith informed us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Uqbah ibn Wassaj: He said: Abu Lubabah was sitting with Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, [and Abu Lubabah said]: Evil is the garment, the garment of pride. So Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Did you hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No. He said: I do not reject your knowledge; I find in the Book of Allah: Whoever wears a garment of pride, Allah will not look at him until he puts it off, even if he has grown old. Then Abu Lubabah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Evil is the heart, the heart of the fat one. So Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No. Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I do not reject your knowledge. I find in the Book of Allah, Exalted: The example of the fat one and the heart of the lean one is like a fat sheep and a lean sheep struck by rain, so it reaches the heart of the lean one and does not reach the heart of the fat one. Then Abu Lubabah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: [Whoever worships Allah], Allah, Mighty and Majestic, suffices him provision. So Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Did you hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No. So he said: I do not reject your knowledge, and I find in the Book of Allah, Exalted: There is no servant who worships Allah, Mighty and Majestic, except that Allah, Exalted, guarantees heaven and earth with his provision until he dies, or as long as he lives."
"2640","2210 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us: He said: I heard Ziyad al-Numayri narrating from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: While a man from those before you went out in two cloaks, and behaved proudly in them, Allah commanded the earth, so it seized him, and he sinks in it until the Day of Resurrection."
"2641","2211 - Abu Bakr said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Exalted, does not look at one who trails his lower garment."
"2642","2212 - al-Harith said: Dawud al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: Both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Whoever wears a garment and behaves proudly in it will be swallowed from the edge of Hell, and will rumble in it as long as the heavens and the earth endure; for Qarun wore a suit and behaved proudly, so he was swallowed, and he rumbles in it until the Day of Resurrection."
"2643","2213/1 - Ibn Abi Umar and Ahmad ibn Mani' both said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Rishdin ibn Kurayb narrated to us, from his father: He said: He heard al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, walking in the lane of Abu Lahab, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man came walking in two cloaks of his, having let his lower garment hang, looking at his sides, strutting, when Allah, Exalted, caused the earth to swallow him, and he sinks in it until the Day of Resurrection. Thus Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated it, but Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi differed from him, narrating it from Rishdin ibn Kurayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2644","2213/2 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Kufi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Ibn Kurayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: I was leading him in the lane of Abu Lahab, and he said: O Kurayb! Have we reached such-and-such place? I said: You are now at it. So he said: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me: He said: While I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this place, a man came strutting in two cloaks, looking at his sides, self-conceited, when the earth swallowed him in this spot, and he sinks in it until the Day of Resurrection."
"2645","2214 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ubaydah ibn Humayd narrated to us, al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, from Ibrahim, freedman of Banu Hashim, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: A bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he mentioned the hadith, and at its end: And beware of trailing the lower garment, for trailing the lower garment is from pride. I say: The hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is in the Sahih with other than this wording."
"2646","2215 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il: He said: Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man trailing his lower garment, so he said: Lift your lower garment. He said: I am thin in the shanks."
"2647","2216/1 - Hushaym narrated to us. 2216/2 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us - both from Husayn, from Abu al-Hajjaj, from Sa'id al-Thaqafi, from a man of his people: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man trailing his lower garment, so he said to him: Lift your lower garment, for Allah does not love the proud. He said: O Messenger of Allah, there is thinness in my shank. He said: That is uglier than what is in your shank."
"2648","2217 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad said in Ziyadat al-Zuhd: Ali ibn Muslim narrated to us, Sayyar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Umar, freedman of Sallam ibn Abi Muti', narrated to us: He said: I heard Bakr ibn Abdillah al-Muzani in the mosque of Basra saying: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who wore [fine clothes] did not criticize those who did not wear, and those who did not wear did not criticize those who wore."
"2649","5 - Chapter on the desirability of displaying blessing when there is no extravagance or pride. 2218 - al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Aslam al-Minqari, from Zuhayr ibn Abi Alqamah: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man of bad appearance, so he said: Do you have wealth? He said: Yes, from all kinds of wealth. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then let its trace be seen upon you, for Allah, Exalted, loves to see the trace of blessing upon His servant, and hates misery and feigned misery."
"2650","2219 - Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Imran ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from his father, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Beautiful and loves beauty; He loves to see the trace of His blessing upon His servant."
"2651","6 - Chapter on the desirability of leaving luxury and soft living. 2220 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id, from his father, from a man of Aslam called Ibn al-Adra', may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Live like Ma'add, be hardy, shoot arrows, and walk barefoot."
"2652","2221/1 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Muslim al-A'war narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had a cotton shirt, short in length and short in sleeves."
"2653","2221/2 - Abd said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, Khalid narrated to us with it."
"2654","2221/3 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, from Muslim, with it."
"2655","2222 - Musaddad said: Abdullah narrated to us, from Zayd Abu Usamah, from Sa'id: He said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, bought two Sunbulani Anbijani shirts for seven dirhams, and clothed Qanbar with one of them. And when he wanted to wear, his lower garment was patched with a patch of leather."
"2656","7 - Chapter on forbidding covering walls. 2223 - Musaddad said: Bishr - he is Ibn al-Mufaddal - narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdillah: He said: I married at the time of my father, and invited the people, and Abu Ayyub was among those invited, and they had covered [the walls] with a green curtain. So Abu Ayyub came and entered - while my father was standing - and looked and saw the house covered with a green curtain, so he said: O Abdullah, do you cover walls? So my father said - feeling shy - O Abu Ayyub, the women overcame us. So he said: Whom I feared the women would overcome, I did not fear they would overcome you. Then he said: By Allah, I will not eat your food, nor enter your house. Then he left."
"2657","2224 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Ja'far al-Khatmi, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b: He said: Abdullah ibn Yazid was invited to food, and when he came he saw the house adorned with curtains, so he sat outside and wept. So it was said to him: What makes you weep? So he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned the hadith. He said: One day he saw a man who had patched his cloak with a piece of fur, so he faced the sunrise and said thus with his hand - Hammad described with his hands, with the palms inward, extending his hands - The world has risen upon you, that is, it has come, until we thought it would fall upon us; and one of you goes in the morning in one suit and returns in the evening in another, and you cover your houses as you cover the Ka'bah. Abdullah ibn Yazid said: How should I not weep when I see you covering your houses as you cover the Ka'bah."
"2658","8 - Chapter on forbidding a woman to wear what describes her bones. 2225/1 - Musaddad said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: A suit and a Syrian garment came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he clothed me with the suit, and clothed Usamah, may Allah be pleased with him, with the garment. So I went in my suit, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Usamah: What did you do with your garment? He said: I clothed my wife with it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then order her to wear beneath it a thin garment that does not describe the size of her bones to men. 2225/2 - Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated it, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibn Usamah, from his father."
"2659","9 - Chapter on designing for a woman; dyeing her hand. 2226 - Musaddad said: Isa narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Rafi' narrated to us, a man of Banu Sulaym narrated to me, from his father, from his grandmother, may Allah be pleased with her: She said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her while she was dyeing [her hand], so he said: Why not, O mother of so-and-so, thus - upon the back of his palm - meaning the design."
"2660","10 - Chapter on dislike of khaluq [saffron perfume] and its permissibility for the married. 2227 - Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah informed us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrated to us: He said: I heard Abu Habibah narrating from the man who came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said: I asked him while I was smeared with khaluq, so he said to me: Go and wash. So I went and washed, then returned, and he did the like of that three times. He said: So I went and fell into a well, and took a pumice - meaning a stone - and began seeking - meaning ablution - then I washed, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Bring your need. I say: Abu Dawud transmitted its meaning from the hadith of Ammar ibn Yasir."
"2661","2228 - Abu Bakr said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Bakr Abdullah ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn Suhayb, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three whom the angels do not approach: one smeared with khaluq, and the drunk, and the one in major impurity."
"2662","2229 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Imran ibn Zibyan, that he heard a man of Banu Hanifah saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I went with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Jews of Banu Qaynuqa', and he saw a man smeared with khaluq, so he withheld his hand. So I said: O Messenger of Allah, perhaps he is a bridegroom. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even so; go and wash it, then scrub it, then wash it, then scrub it [then wash it, then scrub it]."
"2663","2230 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Abu Haywah Shurayh ibn Yazid al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Imran ibn Bishr al-Hadrami: He said: I saw Abdullah ibn Busr, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with a yellow turban upon him, and a yellow cloak."
"2664","2231 - Abu Ya'la said: Mus'ab ibn Abdillah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Isma'il ibn Abdillah ibn Ja'far, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with two garments dyed with saffron upon him: a cloak and a turban."
"2665","2232/1 - Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Hurayth narrated to us, from Mudrik ibn Umarah, from his father: He said: He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah to pledge him, and he saw his hand smeared with khaluq, so he withheld his hand. So a man said to him: May your mother mourn you; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only withheld his hand from you because it is smeared. So he washed his hand, then came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pledged him."
"2666","2232/2 - al-Harith said: Zakariyya ibn Adi narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated to us with it."
"2667","11 - Chapter on dyed [saffron] garments for children and others. 2233 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata and Ibn Abi Mulaykah: He said: We used to enter upon A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, while we were lads, wearing saffron-dyed clothes."
"2668","2234 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Umarah ibn Zad han narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Numayri, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: A youth came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), [wearing a saffron-dyed sheet, so the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said]: If this were under your family's cooking pot, it would be better for you. So the lad went, then came in the morning to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: What did you do with your garment? He said: I did what you ordered me. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I did not order you with that; why did you not cast it upon one of your women."
"2669","12 - Chapter on tattooing. 2235 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim: He said: I entered upon Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was ill, and I saw Asma bint Umays, may Allah be pleased with her, fanning [from him], her hands tattooed [that is, patterned with henna]."
"2670","13 - Chapter on forbidding sitting on the skins of predators. 2236 - al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn al-Shaybani narrated to us, Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan narrated to us, from Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade spreading the musuk of predators as furnishings. Musuk is the plural of mask - with fatha on the mim - meaning skins."
"2671","14 - Chapter on the prohibition of silk for men, its permissibility for women, and the permissibility of selling it to one for whom wearing it is lawful. 2237 - Abu Ya'la said: Ja'far ibn Humayd narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Iyad narrated to us, from his father, from Qays ibn al-Nu'man - and he was a neighbor of mine who completed the Qur'an in the time of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: Cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and Ukaydir of Dumah heard of them, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: Then Ukaydir brought out a qaba [long coat] woven with gold, from what Kisra used to clothe them with. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go back with your qaba, for no one wears this in this world except he will be deprived of it in the Hereafter. So he went back with it, and when he came to his dwelling he felt it hard that his gift be returned to him, so he returned and said: O Messenger of Allah, we are a household upon whom it is hard that our gift be returned, so accept my gift from me. So he said: Go and hand it to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had heard what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about it, so he wept and his eyes shed tears, and thought misery had befallen him. So he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Has anything new happened concerning me? I said about this qaba what I heard, then you sent it to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed until he placed his hand upon his mouth, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I did not send it to you to wear it, but to sell it and benefit from its price."
"2672","2238 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Fakhitah: He said: Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me that a striped silk suit was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent it to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. So he went in the evening wearing it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: I am not pleased for you except with what I am pleased with for myself; I did not clothe you with it to wear it, I clothed you with it to make it headcovers among the Fatimahs. In its chain is weakness, and it has a corroboration from the hadith of Ali himself, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2673","2239 - al-Tayalisi said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, Umm al-Mughirah, freedwoman of the Ansar, informed me: She said: I asked A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about silk worn by women. She said: We used to be clothed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with garments called al-sira [striped silk]."
"2674","2240 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from al-Muhajir ibn Shimas, from his uncle: He said: I was with Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, when two sons came wearing two shirts of silk; he tore them off them and said: This is but for women, and not for men."
"2675","2241 - Abu Bakr said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abbad narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, Thabit ibn Zayd ibn Thabit ibn Zayd ibn Arqam informed me, Unaysah bint Zayd ibn Arqam informed me, from her father, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gold and silk are lawful for the females of my community, unlawful for its males."
"2676","2242 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Muhajir, from Abu al-Aliyah, that Sa'd ibn Malik entered upon Ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was reclining upon a couch of sundus [fine silk], so he said: That I sit upon the coals of ghada [wood] is more beloved to me than that I sit upon this."
"2677","15 - Chapter on the permissibility of wearing silk for an excuse, and the indication of its dislike for children. 2243 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me: He said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf entered with his son Muhammad with him, wearing a shirt of silk. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood, took hold of its collar and tore it. So Abd al-Rahman said: May Allah forgive you; you have frightened the child and startled his heart. He said: Do you clothe them with silk? He said: Then I wear silk, for they are like you."
"2678","2244 - Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, Abu Bakr ibn Hafs narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah: He said: Ibn Awf entered upon Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wearing a silk shirt. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has been mentioned to me that whoever wears silk in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter. Abd al-Rahman said: I hope to wear it in this world and the Hereafter. This chain is sahih."
"2679","2245 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, from Abu Janab al-Kalbi, from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman: He said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of abundant lice, so he said: O Messenger of Allah, do you permit me to wear a shirt of silk? So he permitted him. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed away, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood [as caliph], he came to him with his son and Abu Salamah, wearing a shirt of silk. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What is this? Then he put his hand in the collar of the shirt and tore it to its bottom. So Abd al-Rahman, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made it lawful for me? He said: He made it lawful for you only because you complained of lice; as for other than you, then no."
"2680","16 - Chapter on the excellence of white over others, and wearing all colors. 2246 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Khirash narrated to us, al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear a white cap."
"2681","2247 - Abdan narrated to us, Abu Sinan narrated to us: He said: I saw al-Hasan white in head and beard, wearing a white quilted cap. And the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, in the chapter has preceded in adornment for Friday."
"2682","17 - Chapter on the place of the lower garment. 2248 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Musa ibn Tharwan, from Abu al-Mutawakkil: That he saw Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, his lower garment to mid-shin, his shirt above that, and his cloak above the shirt."
"2683","2249 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father: He said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, his lower garment was to mid-shins. So it was said to him about that, so he said: Such is the waist-wrap of our Companion - meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2684","2250 - Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwah, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah ibn Hunayn, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When your lower garment is wide, wrap yourself with it, and when it is narrow, wear it as a waist-wrap. Ishaq is abandoned."
"2685","18 - Chapter on women's trains. 2251 - Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made one of his women stand, measured a span or two spans from her train, and said: Do not add over this."
"2686","19 - Chapter on gold jewelry. 2252 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Abu Hazim, from Urwah, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The two reds destroyed them: gold and saffron."
"2687","2253 - al-Mu'tamir narrated to us: He said: I heard my father saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Sirin saying: A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, forbade gold and silver vessels, and they kept pressing her until she permitted gold."
"2688","2254 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Umarah, from Zaynab bint Nubayt ibn Jabir: She said: Abu Umamah As'ad ibn Zurarah, may Allah be pleased with him, entrusted my mother and my maternal aunt to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then jewelry came to him containing gold and pearls, called al-ri'ath. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adorned them from those ri'ath. I saw that jewelry with my family."
"2689","20 - Chapter on trimming the mustache and leaving the beard full. 2255 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: He said: A man of the non-Arabs came to the mosque, having left his mustache full and cut his beard short. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said [to him]: What drove you to this? He said: My lord commanded me with this. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Exalted, commanded me to leave my beard full and trim my mustache close."
"2690","21 - Chapter on the dislike of plucking gray hair. 2256 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, and from Yunus, from al-Hasan, and from a shaykh, from al-Sha'bi: He said: They used to dislike plucking gray hair."
"2691","22 - Chapter on dyeing the hair of the beard. 2257/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, Abu Aqil narrated to us: He said: He saw some hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dyed with henna. He said: We used to rinse it with water, and drink that water."
"2692","2257/2 - And with it to Haywah, al-Walid Abu Uthman informed me, from Abu Malik: That he saw some hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dyed with henna, not intensely red. He said: And all of us wash it with water."
"2693","2258 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ijli narrated to us, Ali ibn Abi Sarah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas: That a man with white head and beard entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you not a Muslim? He said: Yes indeed. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then dye."
"2694","2259 - Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Di'amah narrated to us, from Umar ibn Sharik, from his father, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dye with henna, for it is pleasant in scent and calms dizziness. Abu Ya'la said: I do not know whether this Sharik is Ibn Abi Namir or not. And al-Bazzar transmitted it from his path, Thumamah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, with the wording: For it increases your youth and marriage."
"2695","2260 - al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim, tutor of al-Mahdi, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever changes whiteness to black, Allah, Exalted, will not look at him on the Day of Resurrection. Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah is al-Azrami, very weak."
"2696","2261 - al-Tayalisi said: Abd al-Jalil ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Amr ibn Abasah, mentioning the hadith: Gray hair is light. And he added: Except one who dyes it or plucks it. So I said to Shahr: They dye yellow and dye with henna. He said: Yes, as if he means black."
"2697","23 - Chapter on the excellence of one who grows gray in Islam and does not dye. 2262 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, from Abd al-Samad, Salim Abu Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from his grandmother Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her: She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Whoever grows a gray hair in Islam, it will be light for him, so long as he does not change it."
"2698","2263/1 - Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Thabit ibn Ajlan narrated to me, Mujahid ibn Jabr al-Makki narrated to me: He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever grows the gray hair of Islam, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"2699","2263/2 - Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Dimashqi informed us, from Thabit ibn Ajlan, from Mujahid: He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used not to change his gray hair. So it was said to him about that: Why do you not change, when Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to change? So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grows a gray hair in Islam, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and I will not change my gray hair."
"2700","24 - Chapter on kohl. 2264 - al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2701","2265 - And from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: We waited for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come out to us in Ramadan. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out from the house of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she had applied kohl to him and filled his eye with kohl. Amr ibn Khalid is frail."
"2702","25 - Chapter on the ring. 2266 - Ishaq said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Fudayk al-Madani informed us, Hammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Humayd, from a trustworthy man of the people of Makkah, from Aqil, may Allah be pleased with him, that he wore a ring on his right, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore a ring on the right."
"2703","2267 - Ahmad ibn Ayyub informed us, from Abu Hamzah, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Aqil: He said: Aqil, may Allah be pleased with him, killed on the day of Mu'tah a man of the polytheists, and took his ring and a slavegirl with him. So he brought them to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he took the ring and placed it on his finger, then said: Were it not for this image. He said: So he gave Aqil, may Allah be pleased with him, his ring and his slavegirl as spoils."
"2704","2268 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Arubah, Qatadah narrated to me, from Qaza'ah, from Abd al-Rahman, keeper of the water supply: He said: Ziyad entered upon Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, with a gold ring on his hand. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You have taken rings of gold. Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, said: But my ring is of iron. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: That is fouler and more evil; whoever of you wears a ring, let him wear a ring of silver."
"2705","2269 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, from Jarir, from Mansur, from Mujahid: He said: A woman used to make buttons for the sleeve of her shirt, placing them on her finger, covering the ring with them."
"2706","2270 - al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abu Atiq, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wear a ring on his right hand."
"2707","2271 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Awwam, from Azhar ibn Rashid: He said: When Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to his companions a hadith they did not understand, they went to al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, and he explained it to them. One day al-Hasan explained to them the hadith: Do not engrave Arabic upon your rings - that is: Do not engrave Muhammad."
"2708","2272 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from the son of Hudhayfah's daughter, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The engraving of Hudhayfah's ring, may Allah be pleased with him, was two cranes facing each other, and between them: Praise be to Allah."
"2709","2273 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Imran ibn Musa ibn Talhah, from his father: He said: Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, had a ring of gold."
"2710","2274 - Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhajir: He said: I saw upon the hand of Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, a ring of gold."
"2711","2275 - Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Tawus: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the pulpit wearing a gold ring. So he said to the people: A glance to you and a glance to it - and he threw it away."
"2712","2276 - Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Suhayl, from Ibn Umar: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade al-qassiyyah, and al-mitharah, and the gold ring, and al-mufaddam. Yazid said: So I said to al-Hasan: What is al-qassiyyah? He said: Striped garments with silk made in Egypt; I have seen them. I said: What is al-mufaddam? He said: That saturated with saffron dye."
"2713","2277 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Ibrahim al-Taymi: He said: They used to permit the boy to wear a gold ring, then when he reached maturity he cast it off."
"2714","26 - Chapter on sandals. 2278 - Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father: He said: A man narrated to me: He said: I saw the sandal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a raised back, having two straps."
"2715","2279 - al-Harith said: Ashhal narrated to us, Ziyad Abu Umar narrated to us: He said: We entered upon an old man called Muhajir, while I had sandals with two straps. He said: I had left them due to their fame. So he said: What is this? So I said: I meant to leave them due to their fame. So he said: Do not leave them, for the sandal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was thus."
"2716","2280 - Ashhal narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us: He said: I came to a shoemaker in Madinah and said: Sole my sandal. So he said: If you wish I will sole it thus, and if you wish I will sole it as I saw the sandal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). [I said: And you saw the sandal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)]? He said: I saw it in the house of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. He said: I think he said: In the house of Fatimah bint Ubaydillah ibn Abbas. I said: Sole it as I saw the sandal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So he soled it with two straps for it. He said: And I came, and Ibn Sirin had taken it. And the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, on what is said at wearing clothes, has preceded at the beginning of sales."
"2717","27 - Chapter on forbidding decorating swords. 2281/1 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, from Hisham, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Khalid, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade decorating the sword with silver. Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I heard it from one who neither lied nor would lie to me."
"2718","2281/2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us: He said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us with it, and he did not mention Khalid in the chain, and he said: That swords be decorated."
"2719","28 - Chapter on branding animals and castration. 2282/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Tammam, from his grandfather al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade branding upon the face. So al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will brand only at the farthest bone, so he branded at the buttocks."
"2720","2282/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud narrated to us with it."
"2721","2283 - Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Mushir and Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us - separating them - from Ibn Abi Layla, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a donkey branded between its eyes, so he disliked that and said a stern word about it - the wording is Ibn Mushir's - and he added: And he forbade striking the face or branding the face."
"2722","2284 - Waki' narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade castrating horses and beasts. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In it is the growth of creation."
"2723","2285 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from Yahya and Isa, sons of Talhah, from their father: He said: A camel branded upon its face was brought past the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: If only the owners of this camel kept the fire away from this beast. He said: I said: I shall brand at the farthest place from its face. He said: So I branded at the root of the tail."
"2724","24 - Book of sacrifice. 2286 - Musaddad said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Kurz ibn Abi Kathir: He said: Yahya ibn Ya'mar used to give fatwa in Khurasan that when a man buys the sacrifice, names it, and the ten [days of Dhu al-Hijjah] enter, he should refrain from his hair and nails until he sacrifices. Qatadah said: So I mentioned that to Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, so he said: Yes. So I said: From whom? He said: From the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I say: Muslim transmitted it by two other paths from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: From Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2725","2287 - Musaddad said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us: He said: I heard my father saying: Ibn Sirin, when the ten entered, disliked that a man take from his hair, to the point he disliked that children be shaved of hair."
"2726","2288/1 - Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Umarah ibn Abi al-Darda, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: Two young white-spotted rams were gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sacrificed them."
"2727","2288/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with it."
"2728","2289 - Abu Bakr also said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought two white-spotted horned huge castrated rams. So he laid one down and said: In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest; O Allah, from Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. Then he laid the other down and said: In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest; O Allah, from Muhammad and his community, whoever bore witness to You of unity and bore witness to me of conveyance. Its chain is hasan."
"2729","2290 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Hatim ibn Abi Nasr, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best shroud is the suit, and the best sacrifice is the horned ram."
"2730","2291 - Abu Ya'la said: Musaddad narrated to us, Bishr narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif, from Imran: He said: My family would have a thousand sheep, and I would select the young one from them and slaughter it."
"2731","2292/1 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Ayyub Abu al-Ala narrated to us, Abu Sufyan narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that he disliked that any but a Muslim slaughter the sacrifice."
"2732","2292/2 - Hushaym narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, with it. 2292/3 - Hushaym narrated to us, Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim and Layth, from Ata, that both disliked that."
"2733","2292/4 - Hushaym narrated to us, Hajjaj informed us, from Qabus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise."
"2734","2292/5 - Hushaym narrated to us, Layth informed us, from Tawus and Mujahid, and Bayan, from al-Sha'bi, that they disliked that."
"2735","2293 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Amir: He said: I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about the cow and the camel, do they suffice for seven? So he said: And how; does it have seven souls? I said: The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Kufa gave me fatwa. So the people said: Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I did not know."
"2736","2294 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Uqbah ibn Suhban: He said: I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about a man who dedicates a cow: may he sell its skin and give its price as charity? He said: There is no harm in it. Sahih."
"2737","2295 - Abu Bakr said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, in marfu' form: When sacrifice is difficult for you, the young sheep suffices you."
"2738","2296/1 - Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father: He said: A man slaughtered before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed on the day of sacrifice. So he said: It does not suffice you. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, we have a young she-goat? So he said: It suffices you, and it will not suffice anyone after you."
"2739","2296/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Ubaydullah narrated to us with it."
"2740","2297 - Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Qaza'ah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Hajjaj narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Junadah, from Hanash, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when a man came bringing a fat excellent young goat, and a lean worthless young sheep. So he said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a lean worthless young sheep, and this is a fat excellent young goat, and it is the better of them; shall I sacrifice it? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sacrifice it, for Allah loves what is good."
"2741","2298/1 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Humayd, from Thabit and Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from Abu Talhah: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed two white-spotted rams. So he said at slaughtering the first: From Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. And he said at slaughtering the second: From whoever of my community believed in me and affirmed me."
"2742","2298/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us with it."
"2743","2299 - Ahmad ibn Mani' and Abd ibn Humayd both said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zayd - brother of Hammad ibn Zayd - informed us, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from one who informed us with it, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her: Stand, O Fatimah, and witness your sacrifice. Verily for you, with the first drop that falls from its blood, is forgiveness for every sin. Verily it will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with its flesh and blood, seventyfold, then placed in your scale. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is this for the family of Muhammad specifically, for they deserve the good specified for them, or for the family of Muhammad and people generally? He said: Rather for the family of Muhammad and people generally."
"2744","2300 - Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ibrahim - caller of the people of Madinah - narrated to us, from his father: He said: I witnessed Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, at the prayer place saying to two men: Do you not have what you sacrifice with? Both said: No. So he took them to his house and brought out his sheep. He said: May Allah accept from Abu Hurayrah, and from so-and-so and so-and-so. Then he took its liver or something from it, and they ate from it. Then he divided it into three parts; the two men returned with two-thirds, and a third entered the house of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2745","2301 - Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Uqayl ibn Talhah, from Abu al-Khasib: He said: A man asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about sacrifice. So he said: Dislike and avoid the one-eyed whose blindness is clear, and the lame whose lameness is clear, and the sick whose sickness is clear, and the lean whose leanness is clear."
"2746","2302 - Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi: He said: Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, attended the season [of Hajj] and did not sacrifice."
"2747","2303 - And from Fudayl ibn Iyad, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to perform Hajj and not sacrifice. Ibrahim said: And they used to perform Hajj with their provisions and gold, yet not sacrifice."
"2748","1 - Chapter on the aqeeqah and what is done with the newborn. 2304/1 - Abu Bakr said: Shababab narrated to us, from Mughirah ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he performed aqeeqah for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2749","2304/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"2750","2305 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Harith ibn Miskin narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed aqeeqah for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, with two rams. al-Bazzar said: We do not know anyone who corroborated Jarir ibn Hazim upon it."
"2751","2306 - Abu Bakr said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham, from Abbad ibn Hamzah ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: She said: I brought Ibn al-Zubayr to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he tahneeked him with a date and said: This is Abdullah, and you are Umm Abdullah."
"2752","2307 - Abu Ya'la said: Jabbarah narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated to us, from Marwan ibn Salim, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, from al-Husayn - and once he said from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has a child born to him and calls the adhan in his right ear and the iqamah in his left ear, Umm al-Sibyan [evil spirits] will not harm him."
"2753","2308 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the aqeeqah: for the boy two rams, and for the girl one ram."
"2754","2309 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: She said: For the boy two equivalent sheep are sacrificed, and for the girl one sheep. A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed aqeeqah for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, with two sheep each on the seventh day, and ordered that harm be removed from his head. And he said: Slaughter upon the name of Allah and say: In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest; O Allah, from You and to You, this is the aqeeqah of so-and-so. She said: In the pre-Islamic age a piece of cotton would be taken and placed in the blood of the aqeeqah, then placed upon his head. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that khaluq be placed in place of blood."
"2755","25 - Chapters on slaughtered animals. 1 - Chapter on the slaughter of the fetus. 2310 - Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from Layth, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: He said: The fetus is slaughtered until what is in it of blood comes out."
"2756","2311 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the fetus (inside the slaughtered mother) and he said: Its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother."
"2757","2 - Chapter on slaughtered animals. 2312 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salih, the freedman of al-Taw'amah, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: It is disliked that the blade be sharpened while the sheep is looking."
"2758","2313 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abdan narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, [who said]: He saw no harm in the slaughter of the uncircumcised."
"2759","2314 - And Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub, from Abu Hamzah, from Jabir - that is al-Ju'fi - from Abu Ubaydah, from Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I asked Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, about slaughtered animals and he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when one of our riding camels became refractory, and he stabbed it with the sword in its temple."
"2760","3 - Chapter on pronouncing the Name (tasmiyah). 2315 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan that he used to say at slaughter: In the Name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest; O Allah, for You and from You; accept from so-and-so. And Ibn Sirin used to say whatever he wished, then when it was the slaughter he said: In the Name of Allah."
"2761","2316 - And Musaddad said: al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, from his father, who said: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, when he slaughtered would say: In the Name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest."
"2762","2317 - And al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The slaughter of the Muslim is lawful even if he did not pronounce the Name, so long as he did not omit it deliberately; and the slave is likewise."
"2763","2318 - And Musaddad said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from al-Salt, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The slaughter of the Muslim is lawful, whether he mentioned the Name of Allah Almighty or not; and indeed if he mentions, he mentions none but the Name of Allah."
"2764","2319 - And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Abu al-Sha'tha': Ayn informed me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: If the Muslim slaughters and forgets to mention the Name of Allah Almighty, let him eat, for indeed in the Muslim there is a name from among the Names of Allah Almighty. He said: By his saying 'Ayn informed me' he means Ikrimah."
"2765","2320 - Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Dahhak narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, Matar narrated to us, from Talhah, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dedicates the call (of sacrifice) to other than Allah, upon him is the curse of Allah; and whoever says of me what I did not say, likewise... the hadith."
"2766","4 - Chapter on the lawfulness of what has no fang. 2321 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim that he had a fever and fox meat was prescribed for him, but he disliked it and said: It is a beast of prey."
"2767","2322 - Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Habib al-Mu'allim, from Abu al-Muhazzam, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a mastigure (dabb) in a platter [and he said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)]: Eat, for I find it distasteful."
"2768","2323 - And Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Maymunah - and she is his maternal aunt - may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: We were given a mastigure as a gift and I cooked it; then two men of her people came in and she offered it to them; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in while they were eating, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand then lifted it, and said: What is this? She said: A mastigure given to me as a gift, and I cooked it. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put it aside, and the two men were about to put down what was in their hands, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Eat, for you are people of Najd who eat it, and we are people of Tihamah who find it distasteful."
"2769","2324 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Asbat narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Yazid al-Asamm, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I do not eat meat from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not eat."
"2770","2325 - And Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Husayn, a man of Banu Fazarah, from Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was delivering the sermon, and interrupted his sermon, saying: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the mastigure? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A nation of the Children of Israel was transformed, and I do not know which of the animals was transformed."
"2771","2326 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from al-Bara' ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a mastigure, and he said: A nation that was transformed."
"2772","2327 - Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, al-Jurayri Sa'id ibn Iyas narrated to us, from Abu al-Ala', who said: The mastigure was eaten at the table of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he did not eat it and did not forbid [it]."
"2773","5 - Chapter on killing dogs. 2328/1 - Abu Dawud said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, from the daughter of Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, the spear and ordered him to kill the dogs of Madinah; so he killed them except one dog, then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he ordered him to kill it."
"2774","2328/2 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us with this chain."
"2775","2328/3 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Harun al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Yahya ibn [Abi] Kathir narrated to us, from the daughter of Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"2776","2329/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, Aban ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Salma Umm Rafi', from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Gabriel, peace be upon him, came and sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he permitted him but he delayed; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his cloak and stood up to him while he was standing at the door; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have permitted you. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, but we do not enter a house in which there is an image or a dog. So they looked and found a puppy in one of their houses. Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me in the morning, and I did not leave a dog in Madinah but I killed it; then there was a woman in a distant quarter with a dog barking for her, and it was as if I pitied her and left it, and I came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him; so he ordered me to kill it, and I returned to the dog and killed it. He said: The people said: What is lawful for us, O Messenger of Allah, of this species that you ordered to be killed? Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sent down: {And what you have taught of hunting animals, training them}."
"2777","2329/2 - Abu Ya'la narrated it from Abu Bakr at length."
"2778","2329/3 - He said: And al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us with its completion, and he added: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man sends his dog and mentions the Name of Allah, let him eat, so long as it has not eaten (of the game)."
"2779","2330/1 - And al-Harith said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn Abi Khidash narrated to us, from al-Fadl ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Rafi', kill every dog in Madinah. He said: I found some women in Madinah at al-Baqi' who had a dog, and they said: O Abu Rafi', the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has sent our men on campaign, and this dog benefits us after Allah Almighty; by Allah, no one can come to us unless one of our women stands and comes between him and it; so mention it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Abu Rafi' mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: O Abu Rafi', kill it, for it is Allah Almighty who protects them."
"2780","2330/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Rawh narrated to us with its completion. 2330/3 - He said: And Amr ibn al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj with this chain."
"2781","2331 - And al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abu Amir narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Rijal, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to kill the dogs; so I went out and did not see a dog but I killed it; then there was a dog going round, and I went to kill it when a person called to me from inside a house: What do you want to do? I said: I am killing this dog. She said: I am a woman with an estate, and this dog protects me from predators and alerts against the traitor. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he ordered me to kill it."
"2782","6 - Chapter on the prohibition of keeping a dog. 2332 - Abu Ya'la said: Salih ibn Harb narrated to us, Sallam ibn Abi Khubzah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever keeps a dog that is not a dog of livestock or a hunting dog, two qirats are deducted from his reward every day."
"2783","7 - Chapter on the wolf. 2333 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from a man of Banu al-Harith ibn Ka'b called Abu al-Awbar, who said: I was sitting with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned a story... He said: Then he began to narrate, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was out one day while we were sitting with him, when the wolf came until it crouched before him, then wagged [its tail]. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is the wolf, and this is the emissary of the wolves; what do you think? Will you set aside for him something of your wealth? The people said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, we will not set aside for him anything of our wealth. Then a man stood up to it and threw a stone at it, so it turned away howling. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The wolf, and what is the wolf - three times."
"2784","8 - Chapter on the bee and the fly. 2334/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from his father, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The lifespan of the fly is forty nights, and all flies are in the Fire except the bee."
"2785","2334/2 - Abu Sa'id narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Anbasah ibn al-As narrated to me, Hanzalah narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, in marfu' form the like of it, but he did not say: except the bee."
"2786","2335/1 - And Abu Ya'la also said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Isma'il narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: All flies are in the Fire except the bee. And Mujahid used to dislike killing bees."
"2787","2335/2 - Abu Talib narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Layth - that is Ibn Abi Sulaym - from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in marfu' form the like of it; and I include: Mujahid said in the report."
"2788","9 - Chapter on the merit of the white rooster. 2336 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Amr ibn Jumay' narrated to us, Aban narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The voice of the rooster and its flapping with its wings is its bowing and its prostration."
"2789","2337 - Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, Amr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and from Aban, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, in marfu' form: The white rooster is my friend, and the friend of my friend, and the enemy of my enemy."
"2790","2338 - Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, Wahb narrated to us, Talhah ibn Amr narrated to us, from one who narrated to him, from Abu Zayd al-Ansari the like of it, and he added: It guards the house of its owner and nine houses around it; and he used to keep it overnight in his house."
"2791","10 - Chapter on what is disliked to eat. 2339 - Ishaq said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd informed us, al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Abu Matar who said: I came out of the mosque, and there was a man calling behind me; I said: Who is this? They said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. So I walked behind him until he came to the fish sellers, and said: Let no floating (dead fish) be sold in our market."
"2792","2340 - al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abu Muhammad al-Qafalani narrated to us, from Hammam ibn Suhayl, from a man he named, who said: I saw Ammar ibn Yasir on the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Where are the butchers? He said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you: Do not eat the entrails. Al-Nadr said: Meaning the spleen. Then he said: Where are the fish sellers? They said: These. So he went until he stopped before them and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to you: Do not eat of the shillawr (a river fish) nor the eel. Al-Nadr said: One of them is al-jirri, and the other is the mar mahi. And the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, about fish is in the commentary of al-Ma'idah."
"2793","2342 - And al-Humaydi and Ibn Abi Umar, both said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Umar ibn Harmalah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I entered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon my maternal aunt Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her; she said to him: Shall we not bring forward for you something that the granddaughter of Umm Atiq gave us as a gift? So she brought him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) roasted mastigures; when he saw them he spat three times and did not eat of them, but ordered us to eat... then he mentioned the hadith. Ahmad, Abu Dawud, and others have brought it through routes from Ali ibn Zayd without this addition."
"2794","2343 - And Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, al-Qasim and Makhul narrated to us, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, eating the domestic donkey and every beast of prey with fangs."
"2795","2344 - Yahya ibn Wadih narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Asim ibn Umar, from Salma bint Nasr, from a man of Banu Murrah who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, most of my wealth is in donkeys; may I partake of them? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does it not graze the open land and eat trees? I said: Yes. He said: Then partake of them."
"2796","2345 - Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Damrah, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Sulayt, from his father Abu Sulayt - and he was a veteran of Badr - that he said: The prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against eating the flesh of donkeys reached us while we were at Khaybar, while the pots were boiling with them, so we overturned them on their faces."
"2797","2346 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Khalid, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, from a man of his people: That he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the flesh of domestic donkeys, and he mentioned to them something of their matter, then permitted them it."
"2798","2347 - And Abu Bakr said: Shabbabah narrated to us, from Mughirah ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the jallalah (animal feeding on filth) - that its meat be eaten or its milk drunk."
"2799","2348 - And Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: If you shoot a bird and it falls from a mountain and dies, do not eat it, for I fear the fall killed it; and if you shoot a bird and it falls into water, do not eat it, for I fear the water killed it."
"2800","2349 - Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from his father, a man of the Ansar narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the two ears of the heart."
"2801","2350 - Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Nafi' narrated to me [saying]: A man informed Ibn Umar that Sa'd eats hyenas, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did not disapprove of that."
"2802","2351 - Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Ata' saw no harm in eating them (hyenas), and said: It is game."
"2803","2352 - Yahya narrated to us, from Bassam, from Ikrimah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the milk of the jallalah sheep, that is, the one that eats dung."
"2804","2353 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Umar, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A cow broke loose upon wine and drank [of it], and they feared for it; so they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Eat; there is no harm in eating it."
"2805","2354 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating every beast of prey with fangs and every bird with talons."
"2806","11 - Chapter on the prohibition of eating food made for ostentation. 2355 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, from Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a very long hadith, in which: Whoever feeds food for show and repute, Allah Almighty will feed him of the pus of Hell, and that food will be fire in his belly until judgment is passed among the people."
"2807","2356 - And Musaddad said: Rib'i ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Jarud say: There was a man of Banu Rabah called Ibn Uthal, and he was a poet; he came to al-Farazdaq with water at the back of Kufah, on condition that each hamstring a hundred camels when they came to the water; when they came, they stood to them with swords cutting their hocks; the people came out on donkeys and mules seeking the meat, while Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, was in Kufah; so he came out on the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), calling: O people, do not eat of their meat, for it was dedicated to other than Allah Almighty."
"2808","26 - Book of Hunting. 1 - Chapter on one who loves hunting. 2357 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I love to go out with this staff of mine to the mountains and hunt wild beasts with it."
"2809","2 - Chapter on the lawfulness of eating what is hunted with the bow, dog, and other than that. 2358 - Ishaq said: Baqiyyah informed us, al-Zubayr ibn Muhammad ibn al-Walid narrated to me, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: A man of the Ansar, I think him Abd al-Rahman, said: I took my bow and struck birds; among them were those whose slaughter I reached, and those whose slaughter I did not reach; then I met Ibn Mas'ud, Zayd ibn Thabit, and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, may Allah be pleased with them, and I began to set aside the slaughtered; they said: What is this? I said: This is what I reached its slaughter of, and this is what I did not reach; so they mixed them all together and said: We heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Eat whatever your bow returns to you."
"2810","2359 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Aban ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Salma Umm Rafi', from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man sends his hunting (animal) then mentions the Name of Allah Almighty, let him eat, so long as it has not eaten (of the game)."
"2811","2360 - And Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan about the hunting of the falcon and the hawk: If they eat, eat."
"2812","2361 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim about the falcon and the hawk: If they eat, eat, for its eating is only its training."
"2813","2362 - Khalid narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Hammad, from Talhah, from Khaythamah who said: The eagle, the hawk, and the falcon are of the hunting animals."
"2814","2363 - Ibrahim ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, saying: I asked Ata' about the mi'rad (blunt arrow) striking with its side. He said: If you strike [with its side], then whatever it strikes, eat."
"2815","3 - Chapter on dividing the game between the one who set his snares for it and the one who took it. 2364 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Masmul narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Mukhawwal al-Bahzi then al-Sulami narrated to us, saying: I heard my father - and he had reached both the Jahiliyyah and Islam - say: I set snares of mine at al-Abwa', and a bird fell in one of the ropes and escaped; so I went out after it and found a man who had taken it, and we disputed over it; then we hastened to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and found him resting at midday at al-Abwa' under a tree, shaded by a leather mat; so we disputed before him, and he judged it between us in halves."
"2816","4 - Chapter on the lawfulness of eating sea game without slaughter. 2365 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Amr ibn Dinar and Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us that they both heard Shurayh say: Everything in the sea is slaughtered. He said: I mentioned that to Ata', and he said: As for birds, I hold that you slaughter them."
"2817","2366 - Isma'il narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr, from a freedman of Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Every creature in the sea Allah Almighty has slaughtered for you, so eat it."
"2818","2367 - And Abu Ya'la said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Every creature of the creatures of the sea and the land that has no blood that flows has no slaughter (required for it)."
"2819","5 - Chapter on naming the sheep barakah (blessing). 2368 - al-Tayalisi said: Ja'far ibn Burd narrated to us, from Umm Salim, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: How many blessings are in your house - meaning the sheep."
"2820","2369 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Abd Allah, [who said]: Umm Rashid, the freedwoman of Umm Hani', narrated to me, saying: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came in to Umm Hani', may Allah be pleased with her, at midday, and she said: Bring food for Abu al-Hasan. She said: So I brought what was in the house; then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Why do I not see with you any barakah? Umm Hani', may Allah be pleased with her, said: Is this not barakah! He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I do not mean this; have you no sheep? I said: No, by Allah, we have no sheep."
"2821","6 - Chapter on locusts. 2370 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Marwan narrated to us, Fa'id Abu al-Warqa' narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We went on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on an expedition in which our provisions ran out, and there was no food we ate except locusts until we returned from our campaign. I say: It is in the two Sahihs and others with the wording: We went on campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on seven campaigns eating locusts."
"2822","2371 - And Musaddad said: [Yahya] narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, Tariq narrated to me, from his mother, who said: We sent to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, asking him about locusts - and he was asleep - and his household said: He sees us eating them but does not eat them nor forbids us."
"2823","2372 - Yahya narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, Zaynab bint Ka'b narrated to me, saying: Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, used to see us eating locusts and would neither command nor forbid us, and we do not know what prevented him - disgust, or dislike."
"2824","2373 - Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim that he would not eat locusts."
"2825","2374 - And al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Amir - that is al-Sha'bi - from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, smacking his lips. I said: What is the matter with you, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: I crave fried locusts."
"2826","2375 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Waqid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Kaysan narrated to us, Ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Locusts became scarce in one of the years of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in which he ruled; so he asked about them and was not informed of anything, and was grieved at that; so he sent a rider to Yemen, a rider to Syria, and a rider to Iraq, asking: Has any locust been seen or not? Then the rider from Yemen came to him with a handful of locusts and cast them before him; when he saw them he said the takbir three times, then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, created a thousand nations, of them six hundred in the sea and four hundred on land; the first thing to perish of these nations is the locust; and when they perish, they follow in succession like beads when their string is cut."
"2827","7 - Chapter on the slaughter of what cannot be slaughtered (in the throat). 2376 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Haram ibn Uthman narrated to us, from the two sons of Jabir, from their father Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A cow of ours became wild; a man went out and struck it below the neck and above where the shoulder meets, and it mounted its kick; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked, and he said: The tame cow, when it takes the state of the wild, is rendered lawful by what renders the wild lawful."
"2828","2377 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ja'far ibn Mihran narrated to us, Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Mahmud ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Jamuh, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We bought a cow in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to share in it; it broke loose from us and resisted us; a freedman of ours called [Dhakwan], with a sword in his hand, faced it while it was roaming at al-Simad; it inclined toward a hill, and when it passed by him he struck it with the sword at the base of its neck or upon its shoulder, tearing it with the sword, and it fell, and we did not reach its slaughter; so I went out, I and Abd Allah ibn Thabit ibn al-Jidh', and we met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned its matter to him; he said: Eat; if anything of these cattle escapes you, restrain it with what you restrain the wild with."
"2829","8 - Chapter on the prohibition of killing house-dwellers (serpents) and the permission to kill snakes. 2378 - Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Makki - and he is al-Qaddah - narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, would continually see small snakes in her house and ordered that one be killed; then she was visited in sleep and it was said to her: Why did you kill Abd Allah the Muslim? She said: Had he been a Muslim he would not have looked upon the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without permission. It was said to her: But you knew that he would only look when you gather your clothes upon you. He said: When morning came she gave twelve thousand in charity."
"2830","2379 - And al-Tayalisi said: Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about killing snakes, and he said: Man and the snake were created alike: if he sees it, it frightens him, and if it bites him, it pains him; so kill them wherever you find them."
"2831","2380 - And al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: One of my companions narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: The snake is the most wicked of wicked things, so kill it."
"2832","9 - Chapter on what to say when one sees a lion or a dog growls at him. 2381 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you see the lion, say the takbir three times, saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; Mightier than everything and Greater; and I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of what I fear and dread. You will be spared its evil, Allah Almighty willing. And when the dog growls at him, let him say: {O assembly of jinn and men, if you are able}... the verse."
"2833","10 - Chapter on the prohibition of hunting birds at night. 2382 - al-Harith said: Hafs ibn Hamzah narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Fatimah bint Ali who said: I heard my father say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not visit birds in their nests by night, for the night is a security for them."
"2834","27 - Book of Foods and Drinks. 1 - Chapter on the merit of feeding food. 2383 - Abu Ya'la said: Surayj narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yazid, from Bakr ibn Khunays, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cares for the hunger of his Muslim brother and feeds him until he is full, and gives him drink until he is quenched, [Allah] forgives him."
"2835","2384 - Ishaq narrated to us: Sharik narrated to us, from Habib ibn Zayd, from Layla who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us, and I brought him food; some of those present withdrew, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is with them? He said: Fasting. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim at whose place food is eaten but the angels bless him."
"2836","3 - Chapter on the bequest of Qays ibn Asim. 2385/1 - Musaddad said: Umayyah - and he is Ibn Khalid - narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, [from Hakim ibn Qays ibn Asim] who said: Qays ibn Asim enjoined his sons at his death, saying: I enjoin upon you fear of Allah Almighty; make your eldest your chief, for when a people make their eldest their chief they succeed their fathers; and when they make their youngest their chief they are scorned among their peers; and upon you is acquiring wealth, for it is a warner of the noble, and by it one is sufficed from the vile; and beware of begging... then he mentioned the hadith."
"2837","2385/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah ibn Muti' narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Asim: then he mentioned a hadith [saying]: When death came to him he called his sons and said: O my sons, take from me, for none is more sincere to you than I; when I die, make your elders your chiefs and do not make your young ones your chiefs, lest people deem your elders foolish and you become lowly to them; and upon you is improving wealth, for it is a warner of the noble and by it one is sufficed from the vile... the hadith."
"2838","2385/3 - And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from [al-Hasan], from Qays ibn Asim... then he mentioned the hadith."
"2839","4 - Chapter on hospitality to the guest and that in every moist liver there is reward. 2386 - Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Abu Yunus, from al-Miqdad, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: If the guest spends the night deprived, it is a duty upon the Muslims to aid him until they take his hospitality from their crops or their udders. Thus he brought it in the musnad of al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad; and its basis is known from the hadith of al-Miqdad ibn Ma'di Karib."
"2840","2387 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Masmuli narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Mukhawwal al-Bahzi narrated to us, from his father, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, we meet camels with milk in them, tied, while we are needy. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call, O owner of the camels, three times; if he comes, else untie its hobbles then drink, then tie and leave for the milk a caller. I said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Establish the prayer, give the zakat, fast Ramadan, perform hajj of the House, perform umrah, be dutiful to your parents, join your kin, honor the guest, command the good, forbid the evil, and turn with the truth wherever it turns. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, stray animals come to us; is there reward for us in giving them drink? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, in every moist liver there is reward."
"2841","5 - Chapter on hospitality. 2388/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Sadaqah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Hospitality is three days; what is beyond that is charity. Let the guest depart from the guest and not burden the household."
"2842","2388/2 - And Musaddad said: Hafs narrated to us, from Layth the like of it. [And] Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Layth the like of it."
"2843","2388/3 - And Abu Ya'la said: Hashim ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr al-Raqqi narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Ziyad ibn al-Mughirah or Ziyad ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, [the like of it], and his wording: The guest has upon the one with whom he alights a right of three; what exceeds is charity; and upon the guest is to depart, not paining the people of his lodging."
"2844","2389 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, Abu Awn narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: Some people of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled and ran out of provisions; they passed by a tribe of Bedouins and asked them for hospitality, but they refused; they asked them to sell, but they refused; so they overpowered them and took of their food; the Bedouins went to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, complaining of them; the Ansar were apprehensive; then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do you withhold from the wayfarer what Allah creates by night and day in the udders of camels and sheep? The wayfarer has more right to the water than the one who built over it."
"2845","6 - Chapter on the etiquettes of eating. 2390 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When you eat, begin with salt and end with salt, for salt is a cure for seventy diseases, the first of them madness, leprosy, vitiligo, toothache, throat pain, and belly pain."
"2846","2391 - And Abu Ya'la said: Sulayman ibn Umar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Wazi', from Salim, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats of these meats anything, let him wash his hand of the smell of its grease, and not harm the one beside him."
"2847","2392 - And al-Harith said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade peeling the fresh date."
"2848","2393 - And Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Isa ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from some people of Makkah, from Abu Salamah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to say when he finished his food: Praise be to Allah who fed us and gave us drink; and praise be to Allah who sufficed us and sheltered us; and praise be to Allah who favored us and bestowed; we ask Him by His mercy to protect us from the Fire, for how many an unsufficed one finds no resting place nor shelter. Al-Bazzar narrated it from Mahmud ibn Bakr, from his father, except that he said: [from] some people of Makkah, narrated by Ibn Abi Najih, saying: We do not know it narrated but with this chain. I say: It has a witness from the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As in the book of Ibn al-Sunni."
"2849","2394 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Shami narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Harb ibn Surayj, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman who said: I supped with Abu Burdah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: Shall I not narrate to you what my father Abd Allah ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats and is full, and drinks and is quenched, and says: Praise be to Allah who fed me and filled me, and gave me drink and quenched me, exits his sins as the day his mother bore him."
"2850","2395/1 - And Musaddad said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Rafi' narrated to us, from Mujahid who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never ate reclining except once; then he sat and said: I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger."
"2851","2395/2 - He said: And Yahya - that is Ibn Sa'id - narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from Abd al-Aziz the like of it."
"2852","2396 - And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Hashim ibn Amir al-Aslami narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Sa'd, from his father, who said: I was the guide of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from al-Arj to Madinah, and I saw him eating reclining."
"2853","2397 - And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Ibn Jud'an narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for me, I do not eat reclining."
"2854","2398 - And Musaddad said: Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu Yahya, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cool the food, for the hot has no blessing in it."
"2855","2399 - Mu'tamir narrated to us, saying: I heard my father narrate from an elder in the gathering of Abu Uthman, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked: Which food is most unlawful or most wicked? He said: That you eat [of] your camel while it looks at you."
"2856","2400 - Isma'il narrated to us, Ibn Awn informed us, from Muhammad, or another of his companions, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was brought khabis (a sweet) in a vessel of silver or gold, and he ordered that it be transferred onto a loaf, then he ate of it."
"2857","2401 - And Abu Ya'la said: Khallad ibn Aslam narrated to us, Abd al-Majid ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved food to Allah [Almighty] is that over which the most hands gather."
"2858","2402/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Uqbah ibn Khalid al-Sakuni narrated to us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, my father informed me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you eat food, remove your sandals, for it is more restful for your feet."
"2859","2402/2 - Abu Ya'la narrated it: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Uqbah narrated to us with this chain."
"2860","2403 - And Abu Ya'la and al-Bazzar together said: Mu'adh ibn Shu'bah narrated to us, Dawud ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us, from Abu al-Haytham, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When food is brought near to one of you while on his feet are sandals, let him remove his sandals; for it is more restful for the feet, and it is of the Sunnah."
"2861","2404 - And al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ata', from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would eat standing and sitting... the hadith."
"2862","2405 - And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, went to the lavatory then came out, and food was brought to him; it was said to him: Will you not perform ablution? He said: I cleanse with my left and eat with my right."
"2863","2406 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah and Urwah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended to eat, would wash his hands."
"2864","2407 - And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of the privy and ate; it was said to him: Will you not perform ablution? He said: Do I intend to pray, that I should perform ablution?"
"2865","2408 - And Abu Ya'la said: Shabab al-Usfuri narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, saying: I heard Musa al-Juhani say: al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud informed me, from his father, from his grandfather Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets to mention Allah Almighty at the beginning of his food, let him say when he remembers: In the Name of Allah, at its beginning and its end; for he meets his food anew, and deprives the wicked of what it would take of it. Ibn Hibban declared it sahih."
"2866","2409 - And he also said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abd Allah ibn Utbah, from a woman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a waterskin, and a Bedouin took it with three morsels; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had he but said: In the Name of Allah, it would have sufficed them; and he said: When one of you forgets the Name of Allah over his food, let him say when he remembers: In the Name of Allah, at its beginning and its end."
"2867","2410/1 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, Abu al-Husayn al-Ukli narrated to us, Fa'id freedman of Abadil narrated to us, my master Ubayd Allah - that is Ibn Abi Rafi' - narrated to me, from his grandmother Umm Rafi', may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I made khazirah (a meat broth) for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he came with two men, and she brought it near to them; then a Bedouin passed, and [the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)] invited him to the food; he took it in one mouthful; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: [Put it down] and mention the Name [of Allah Almighty] and eat. He said: So he ate and was full, and some remained of it."
"2868","2410/2 - Muhammad - that is al-Muqaddami - narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Fa'id freedman of Ubayd Allah ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi' narrated to us, saying: Ubayd Allah ibn Ali narrated to me, from his grandmother [Salma] the freedwoman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with her, [that she] informed him that she made khazirah for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she brought it near to him and he ate it with some of his Companions, and some remainder remained; then a Bedouin passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invited him; the Bedouin took it all with his hand; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Put it down. So he put it down, and he said to him: Say: In the Name of Allah, and take from what is nearest. She said: So he was full of it, and a remainder remained."
"2869","2411/1 - Zuhayr narrated to us, Shabbabah narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Matar, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating standing."
"2870","2411/2 - And al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, Shabbabah narrated to us with this chain."
"2871","7 - Chapter on vinegar. 2412 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf narrated to us, from Aban, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate vinegar of wine."
"2872","8 - Chapter on cheese. 2413 - Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Amr ibn Mansur narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought cheese, and it was said: This is food that the Magians make. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mention the Name of Allah [over it] and eat. I say: Abu Dawud brought it through the route of Amr ibn Mansur with this chain with another wording."
"2873","2414 - And Musaddad also said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Amr ibn Uthman, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him: That he saw no harm in eating cheese."
"2874","2415 - And Hafs narrated to us, from Hajjaj, from Ata' and Abu Iyad that they both said: There is no harm in the cheese of the Magians."
"2875","9 - Chapter on oil. 2416 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Amr, from al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, eat oil and anoint with oil, for whoever anoints with oil, Satan does not approach him for forty nights."
"2876","2417 - Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Hazrah Ya'qub ibn Mujahid narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, who said: I heard A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say, when oil was mentioned before her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command that it be eaten and anointed with, saying: It is a blessed tree."
"2877","10 - Chapter on melon with fresh dates. 2418 - al-Tayalisi said: Zam'ah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn [Abi Sulayman, from one of the companions of Jabir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat melon with fresh dates, saying: They are both good."
"2878","11 - Chapter on the merit of palm trees. 2419 - Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, Masrur ibn Sa'id al-Taymi narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Urwah ibn Ruwaym, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Honor your aunt the palm tree, for it was created of the clay of which Adam was created; and there is no tree that is pollinated but it; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Feed your women in childbirth fresh dates; and if there are no fresh dates, then dry dates; and there is no tree more noble to Allah than the tree beneath which Maryam bint Imran alighted."
"2879","12 - Chapter on chicory. 2420 - al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Zakariyya al-Hashimi narrated to us, Aban ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat chicory and do not hate it, for there is no day of days but a drop of Paradise falls upon it."
"2880","13 - Chapter on khabis. 2421 - [And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Haytham] ibn Imran narrated to us: My grandfather Abd Allah ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to me, saying: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, made khabis with honey, ghee, and wheat, and brought it in a bowl to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said: What is this? He said: This, O Prophet of Allah, is something the non-Arabs make of wheat, honey, and ghee, calling it khabis. He said: So he ate."
"2881","14 - Chapter on one invited to food who wishes to bring someone with him: let him seek permission. 2422 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, from Abu Maysarah, who said: A man made food for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and invited him, and he said: Do you permit me (to bring) Sa'd? So he permitted him. Then he made food and said: Do you permit me (to bring) Sa'd? [So he permitted him. Then he made food and said: Do you permit (bringing) Sa'd?] for he is your companion."
"2882","15 - Chapter on the obligation of accepting the invitation. 2423 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um al-Ifriqi, from his father, who said: Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you is invited, let him accept, even if fasting."
"2883","16 - Chapter on the voluntary fasting person breaking his fast when invited. 2424/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man made [food and invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions; then a man said:] I am fasting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother made food and invited you; break your fast and make up [a day] in its place."
"2884","2424/2 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Ubayd the like of it."
"2885","17 - Chapter on the dislike of eating in the markets. 2425 - Abd said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Furat narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Luqman narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Eating in the markets is baseness."
"2886","2426 - Abu Ya'la said: Isa ibn Salim narrated to us, Wahb ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he entered the place of ablution and found a morsel or a crust in the channel of stool and urine; so he took it, removed the filth from it, washed it well, then handed it to his servant, saying: O servant, remind me of it when I perform ablution. When he performed ablution he said: Hand me the morsel - or he said: the crust - and he said: O my master, I ate it. He said: Go, for you are free for the sake of Allah. The servant said to him: O my master, for what did you free me? He said: Because I heard my mother Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention from her father the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever finds a morsel or a crust in the channel of stool or urine, takes it, removes the filth from it, washes it well, then eats it - it will not settle in his belly until he is forgiven; so I would not employ a man of the people of Paradise. [Wahb] this is Abu al-Bakhtari the judge, known for lying and fabricating hadith; and this hadith is of what he fabricated; and Ibn al-Jawzi mentioned it in al-Mawdu'at and exposed the matter of this hadith well."
"2887","19 - Chapter on [the etiquettes of] drinking. 2427 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, [Mu'alla ibn Mansur] narrated to us, Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman al-Asbahani narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted [at a dwelling], and a woman sent to him with her son a ewe; so he milked, then [said]: Go with it to your mother, and she drank until she was quenched; then he came with another ewe, and milked, then gave Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, drink; then he came with another ewe, milked, then drank."
"2888","2428 - Sa'id ibn Abi al-Rabi' al-Samman narrated to us, Anbasah informed me, from al-Khasib, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from Abd Allah ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Take comfort in the sheep, for it is the watering of Allah Almighty; and when you milk them, do not exhaust them, and leave the caller of the milk."
"2889","2429 - [Amr narrated to us], Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Masmul narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Mukhawwal narrated to us, saying: I heard my father say: I said: O Messenger of Allah, camels pass by us while we are in drought, full of milk. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Milk and drink, and leave the callers of the milk."
"2890","2430 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah and al-Walid ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Mughith, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would indeed beware of drinking in the filthy vessel."
"2891","2431 - And Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Isma'il, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade drinking from the bent (folded-mouth) vessel."
"2892","2432 - And Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Simak who said: My uncle, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sent me to the Ansar to demand payment from a man of them; then a man of them brought drink in a vessel, and one of them blew into it; the other said: Do not do it, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to forbid that. Sahih."
"2893","2433 - And Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink one day, drinking in three breaths; I said: O Messenger of Allah, you drank the water in three breaths? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, it is more healing, more wholesome, and healthier."
"2894","2434/1 - And al-Harith said: Khalid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Layth, from Ata', from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to drink from the mouth of the waterskin."
"2895","2434/2 - And Abu Bakr said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Hasan, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2896","2435 - And Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us, Amr ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Mahfuz ibn Alqamah, from al-Hadrami - and he was of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked blowing into drink. He said: And he saw a man blowing into drink, then drinking standing; he said: If you can vomit it, then vomit it."
"2897","20 - Chapter on the merit of giving water to drink. 2436 - al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I heard Kudayr al-Dabbi fifty years ago; Shu'bah said: And I heard from Abu Ishaq forty years ago; Abu Dawud said: And I heard it [myself] from Shu'bah forty-five or forty-six [years] ago, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah and said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me of a deed that admits me to Paradise? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Speak justice and give the surplus. He said: If I cannot [do that]? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then feed food [and] spread peace. He said: If I cannot do that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: [Have you any camels? He said: Yes.] He said: Then look to a camel of your camels and a waterskin, and look to a household that drinks water only on alternate days, and give them drink; for [perhaps] your camel will not perish nor your waterskin tear until Paradise becomes due for you."
"2898","21 - Chapter on the dislike of withholding surplus water. 2437 - Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahim al-Sayigh, from a steward of Sa'd, from Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever withholds surplus water, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection."
"2899","22 - Chapter on the much drinking of the disbeliever, as he does not mention the Name of Allah Almighty. 2438 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim al-Sami narrated to us, Hammad - that is Ibn Salamah - narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, from Sa'id ibn Yasar, who said: I saw a man of Juhaynah, never saw a man greater or taller; he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a famine or hardship that struck the people; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Distribute them. A man would take the hand of a man, and a man would take the hand of two men; the people would avoid me for what they saw of my tallness and greatness; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and went with me to his house; he milked a ewe and I drank its milk; then he milked another and I drank its milk, until he milked for me seven; he said: I went; then when it was the morrow I embraced Islam; then I came, and he milked for me one ewe, and I was full and quenched; I said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I never was full nor quenched before today. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer drinks in one intestine, and the disbeliever in seven intestines. This is a sahih hadith; Ahmad brought it from this route, the last of it, without the story at length."
"2900","2439/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, Ubayd al-Agharr narrated to us, from Ata' ibn Yasar, from Jahjah al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He came with a party of his people intending Islam; they attended with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the Maghrib; when he gave the salutation he said: Let each man take the hand of his sitting companion. None remained in the mosque but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I; and I was a tall man, none preferred over me; so the Messenger of Allah took me to his house and milked for me a she-goat, and I finished it [until he milked for me seven she-goats and I finished them]; then I was brought the produce of a cooking pot and I finished it; Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, said: May Allah starve whoever starved the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) this night. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hush, O Umm Ayman; he ate his provision, and our provision is upon Allah Almighty. In the morning they sat; he and his Companions gathered, and each man began to tell of those to whom they had gone; Jahjah said: Seven she-goats were milked for me and I finished them, and a pot was cooked and I finished it; then they prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the Maghrib; he said: Let each of you take the hand of his sitting companion. None remained in the mosque but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and I; and I was great and tall, none preferred over me; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took me to his house and milked for me a she-goat, and I was quenched and full. Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, said: O Messenger of Allah, is this not our guest? He said: [Yes.] The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He ate this night in the intestine of a believer, whereas before that he ate in the intestine of a disbeliever; and the disbeliever eats in seven intestines, and the believer eats in one intestine."
"2901","2439/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr and Abu Kurayb narrated to us, they said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, then he mentioned the text without the story."
"2902","Book of Medicine, 1 - Chapter on the command to seek treatment. 2440 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Talhah narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, seek treatment, for Allah Almighty has not created a disease except that He has created for it a cure, except al-sam. And al-sam is death. I say: Talhah is Ibn Amr, weak, and he contradicted in both its chain and text; al-Bukhari narrated it through Umar ibn Said ibn Abi Husayn, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with the wording of the text: Allah Almighty did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure, without mentioning its beginning or its end."
"2903","2 - Chapter on qist (costus). 2441 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and with her was a boy whose nostrils were gushing blood. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is this? They said: He has al-udhrah (a throat ailment). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why do you torture your children; it suffices one of you to take Indian qist, rub it with water seven times, then pour it into his mouth as a draught. He said: So they did so and he recovered. Its chain is hasan (good)."
"2904","4 - Chapter on the prohibition of sitting in the sun. 2442 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, do not face the sun, for facing it is a disease and turning one's back to it is a cure."
"2905","5 - Chapter on cold water for fever. 2443 - Al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Jarir ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, Yahya ibn Amr al-Naji narrated to me, from Alqamah ibn Abdillah al-Muzani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whichever one of you is seized by al-wird, let him pour over himself a jug of cold water. Al-Hadrami said: Al-wird is fever."
"2906","2444 - Abu Yala said: Harun al-Hammal narrated to us, Rawh narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you has fever, let him sprinkle cold water over himself for three nights before dawn."
"2907","6 - Chapter on talbinah. 2445 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mamar, from Ishaq ibn Abi Talhah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: In talbinah (a barley broth) there is a cure for every disease."
"2908","7 - Chapter on henna. 2446 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Di'amah narrated to us, Umar ibn Sharik narrated to us, from his father, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Dye with henna, for it has a pleasant scent and calms dizziness. Abu Yala said: I do not know whether this Sharik is Ibn Abi Namir or not."
"2909","8 - Chapter on purslane (al-rijlah). 2447 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Aslami narrated to us, from Thawr, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by purslane while he had an ulcer on his foot, so he treated it with it and it healed. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah bless you, grow wherever you wish, for you are a cure for seventy diseases, the least of which is headache."
"2910","9 - Chapter on frankincense, myrrh and thyme. 2448 - Abu Yala said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Ubaydillah ibn Abi Jafar, from Aban ibn Salih, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fumigate your houses with frankincense, myrrh and thyme."
"2911","10 - Chapter on the remembrance that removes sickness. 2449 - Abu Yala said: Bishr ibn Sayhan narrated to us, Harb ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi, from Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with his hand in my hand, and he came upon a man shabby in appearance and said: O father of so-and-so, what has brought you to what I see? He said: Sickness and hardship, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Shall I not teach you words through which Allah will remove sickness and hardship from you? He said: No, nothing would please me with them even if I had been present with you at Badr and Uhud. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed and said: And can the people of Badr and the people of Uhud attain what the contented poor man attains? Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So I said: Teach me, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say, O Abu Hurayrah: I place my trust in the Ever-Living who does not die, all praise belongs to Allah who has not taken a son and has no partner in dominion, to the end of the verse. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me when my condition had improved and said: What is the matter? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I kept saying the words you taught me. Musa is weak."
"2912","11 - Chapter on sesame oil. 2450 - Ishaq said: Waki narrated to us, from Jabir, that is al-Jufi, from Abu Jafar, that is al-Baqir Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) poured sesame oil (into the nose)."
"2913","12 - Chapter on expiations of illness, the reward of the sick, and that the believer is afflicted severely so his reward increases. 2451/1 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah ibn Masud, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he smiled. We said: O Messenger of Allah, what made you smile? He said: I marvel at the believer and his anguish over sickness; if he knew what he has in sickness, he would love to remain sick until he meets his Lord. Then he smiled a second time and raised his head to the sky and looked at it. They said: What made you smile, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I marvelled at two angels who descended from heaven seeking a believer in the place where he used to pray, but did not find him, so they ascended to Allah Almighty and said: O Lord, Your servant so-and-so, we used to record for him such-and-such deeds every day, and You have confined him in Your fetters, meaning sickness. Allah Almighty said to them: Record for My servant the like of what he does every day and night, and do not diminish anything for him; he shall have the reward of what he did plus the reward of that with which I confined him."
"2914","2451/2 - Al-Tayalisi said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us with it. This hadith has a weak chain."
"2915","2452/1 - Ishaq said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Hajjaj, that is Ibn Artah, narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A man may have a rank with Allah Almighty which he cannot reach by deeds until he is afflicted in his body, and so he reaches it through that affliction. This is a weak chain."
"2916","2452/2 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, adding between Jabalah and Ibn Masud: from one who informed him."
"2917","2453 - Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Ashath ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Abu Burdah, from one of the Mothers of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill and it became severe upon him; when he recovered, I said: If one of us did this, it would be feared for her. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not know that the believer is afflicted severely with pain so that his sins are removed from him."
"2918","2454 - Abu Yala said: Abu Dawud Sulayman ibn Muhammad al-Mubaraki narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Dawud al-Sanani, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, who said: He went to the mosque of Damascus early in the heat, and met Shaddad ibn Aws and another with him. He said: Where are you heading, may Allah have mercy on you both? They said: Here, to a sick brother of ours to visit him. He said: So he set out with them until they entered upon that man, and they said to him: How are you this morning? He said: I am in Allah's blessing and bounty. He said: Shaddad said to him: Rejoice in the expiation of evil deeds and the removal of sins, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah Almighty says: When I afflict a believing servant of Mine and he praises Me for what I afflicted him with, then from the day he rises from that bed he is like the day his mother gave birth to him, free of sins; and Allah Almighty says: I am the One who confined this servant of Mine and afflicted him, so record for him what you used to record for him when he was healthy."
"2919","2455 - Abu al-Jahm al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Jabir al-Jufi, from Ziyad al-Numayri, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a tree and shook it until as much of its leaves fell as Allah willed to fall, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pains and calamities remove the sins of the son of Adam faster than I did with this tree."
"2920","2456/1 - Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Fahd ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2921","2456/2 - And Hudbah narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Muslim, the companion of al-Sabiri, narrated to us, and Abu Yasir Ammar ibn Nasr narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Atiyah narrated to us, both from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The likeness of the believer is like the ear of grain, bending at times and standing upright at times."
"2922","2457/1 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Abu Zura narrated to us, Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man may have a station with Allah Almighty which he cannot reach by deeds, so He keeps afflicting him with what he dislikes until He brings him to it."
"2923","2457/2 - Abu Yala said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us with it. And Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"2924","2458 - Al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Ziyad, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim afflicted with a calamity who remembers his calamity after forty years and renews istirja (saying: to Allah we belong and to Him we return) for it, except that Allah gives him in reward at that time the like of what he was given on the day he was afflicted."
"2925","2459 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan who said: I heard my father say: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, visited me while I was sick and overcome, and said: Has your companion prayed? They said: Yes. He said: I have been informed that no servant complains of illness except that Allah Almighty records for him what he used to do while healthy, until Allah Almighty takes him or relieves him."
"2926","2460 - Abu Yala said: Salih ibn Malik narrated to us, Abd al-Ala ibn Abi al-Musawir narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no servant who falls ill with an illness except that Allah commands his two guardian angels: whatever evil deed he does, do not record it, and whatever good deed he does, record it as ten good deeds, and record for him the deeds he used to do while healthy, even if he does not do them."
"2927","13 - Chapter on one whose sight is gone. 2461 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from al-Musayyib ibn Rafi who said: It used to be said: A man's affliction in his sight is like his affliction in his own self."
"2928","2462 - Abu Yala said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Said ibn Sulaym al-Dabbi narrated to us, Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty said: When I take the two precious things of a servant, I am not content with any reward for him less than Paradise. I said: O Messenger of Allah, even if it is one? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even if it is one. Al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, without his saying: even if it is one, to the end, and it is a rejected addition, and there is weakness in Said."
"2929","2463 - Abu Yala said: Yaqub ibn Mahan narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty says: When I take the two precious things of My servant and he is patient and seeks reward, I am not content with any reward for him less than Paradise. Ibn Hibban authenticated it, and al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Awsat through another chain from Hushaym."
"2930","2464 - Abu Yala also said: Abu al-Rabi Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Khuttali al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from Luqman ibn Amir, from Suwayd ibn Jabalah, from al-Irbad ibn Sariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Your Lord Almighty said: When I take from My servant his two precious things, while he is attached to them, I am not content with any reward for them less than Paradise, if he praises Me for them."
"2931","14 - Chapter on censure of one who never falls ill. 2465/1 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from Muslim ibn Aqil, freedman of the Zurqis, who said: I visited Abdillah ibn Iyas ibn Abi Fatimah, and he said: O Abu Aqil, my father narrated to me that his father informed him, saying: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting, he said: Which of you loves never to fall ill? We hastened to him and said: We do, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you love to be like the braying donkeys? And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color until we saw the change in his face. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not love to be people of affliction and expiations? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, the believer is afflicted with affliction, and that is part of his honor with Allah Almighty; he is afflicted with affliction until he attains through it a station with Allah Almighty which he would not attain without being afflicted with that, so Allah Almighty brings him to that station. Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd is weak."
"2932","2465/2 - Abu Bakr said: Musab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, that is Ibn Abi Humayd, narrated to us with it in similar wording."
"2933","2466/1 - Abu Bakr said: Abdillah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Sinan ibn Rabiah al-Hadrami, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a daughter such-and-such - and she mentioned her beauty and loveliness - so I offer her to you in marriage. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have accepted her. She kept praising her until she mentioned that she never had a headache nor ever complained of anything. He said: I have no need of your daughter."
"2934","2466/2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us."
"2935","2467 - Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Uthman, who said: A bulky Bedouin of huge build visited the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: When was your last encounter with fever? He said: I do not know it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And headache? He said: I do not know what it is. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were you ever afflicted in your wealth? He said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were you ever bereaved of a child? He said: No. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah hates the fierce demon-like one who is never bereaved of a child nor afflicted in his wealth."
"2936","15 - Chapter on the virtue of concealing calamity. 2468 - Abu Yala said: Abu Musa al-Harawi narrated to us, Zafir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Part of the perfection of righteousness is concealing calamities."
"2937","16 - Chapter on the virtue of visiting the sick. 2469/1 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yala ibn Ata, from Abdillah ibn Yasar, who said: Amr ibn Hurayth visited al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Do you visit al-Hasan while harboring what is in your heart? Amr said to him: You are not my Lord to turn my heart wherever you wish. He said: That does not prevent us from giving you sincere advice; I heard the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No Muslim visits his brother except that Allah Almighty sends for him seventy thousand angels... the hadith."
"2938","2469/2 - Al-Harith said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us with it."
"2939","2470 - Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us, Ishaq narrated to us, Muawiyah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Maysarah, from Abu Idris, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When a man goes out intending to visit a believing brother, he wades through mercy up to his waist; and when he sits with the sick person and settles seated, mercy envelops him."
"2940","2471 - Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Walid ibn Qays, who said: Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever coincides with fasting on Friday, visits a sick person, attends a funeral, and frees a slave, Paradise becomes due for him."
"2941","2472 - Abu Bakr and Abd ibn Humayd both said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Qays Abu Umarah narrated to me, saying: I heard Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Hazm narrating from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever visits a sick person keeps wading through mercy, until when he sits he is immersed in it. Abd added: Then when he returns he keeps wading through it until he returns to where he came from."
"2942","2473 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned the hadith at length, and in it: Whoever visits a sick person, for every step he takes until he returns to his house there are seventy thousand good deeds, the erasure of seventy thousand evil deeds, the raising of seventy thousand degrees, and seventy thousand angels are assigned to him who visit him and seek forgiveness for him until the Day of Resurrection; whoever attends to a sick person for a day and night, Allah Almighty will raise him with His close friend Ibrahim, peace be upon him, until he crosses the bridge like flashing lightning; and whoever strives to fulfill a need for a sick person emerges from his sins like the day his mother gave birth to him. A man from the Ansar said: What if the sick person is his relative or part of his family? He said: And who has greater reward than one who strives to fulfill the need of his family? This hadith is fabricated."
"2943","2474/1 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Jahm al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Abbad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed a man from among his brothers for three days, he would ask about him; if he was absent he would supplicate for him, if he was present he would visit him, and if he was sick he would visit him in illness. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed a man from the Ansar and asked about him on the third day. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, we left him like a chick, nothing enters his head except that it exits from his backside. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to some of his Companions: Visit your brother. So we went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to visit him, and among the people were Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. When we entered, he was as described to us. He said: How do you find yourself? He said: Nothing enters my head except that it exits from my backside. He said: And what caused that? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I passed by you while you were praying Maghrib, so I prayed with you while you were reciting this surah: Al-Qariah, what is al-Qariah, to its end. So I said: O Allah, whatever sin of mine You will punish me for in the Hereafter, hasten its punishment for me in this world; so you see me as you see me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What an evil thing you said; why did you not ask Allah Almighty to grant you good in this world and good in the Hereafter and to protect you from the punishment of the Fire. He said: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him and he supplicated with that, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated. He said: So he stood up as if released from bonds. When we went out, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, you just exhorted us to visit the sick, so what is our reward in that? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a Muslim man leaves his house to visit his Muslim brother, he wades through mercy up to his waist; when he sits with the sick person, mercy envelops him and envelops the sick person; the sick person is in the shade of Allah Almighty's Throne, and the visitor is in the shade of His sanctity; and Allah Almighty says to His angels: Look how long the visitors stayed with the sick person. They say: O Lord, a moment, if it was a moment. Then He says to His angels: Record for My servant the worship of a thousand years. If they stayed an hour, He says: Record for him an eternity, and eternity is ten thousand years. If he dies before that, he enters Paradise; if he lives, not a single sin is recorded against him. If it is morning, seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until evening, and he is among the fruits of Paradise; and if it is evening, seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until morning, and he is among the fruits of Paradise."
"2944","2474/2 - Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to visit a man in illness and mentioned it... and added: So he said it and was cured. I say: The beginning of the hadith is in al-Sahih in meaning but not in this wording, and from Umar's question, may Allah be pleased with him, to its end, Abbad ibn Kathir alone narrated it, and he is weak, and the traces of fabrication are apparent upon it."
"2945","17 - Chapter on the prohibition of entering a land in which plague has occurred. 2475/1 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad informed us."
"2946","2475/2 - Abu Bakr said in his Musannaf: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, saying: Urwah narrated to me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Abdillah ibn Umar, who said: Umar wrote to his governors in Syria: When you hear that the epidemic has been lifted, write to me. Then I came to him while he was asleep, after his return from Sargh, and I heard him when he woke from his sleep saying: O Allah, forgive me for returning here from Sargh."
"2947","2476 - Abd said: Umar ibn Said al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Makhul, from Umm Ayman, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: She heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advising one of his family, saying: If death befalls the people while you are among them, then remain firm. The hadith is disconnected (munqati)."
"2948","18 - Chapter on moving away from an unhealthy land. 2477 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mamar, from a man of the family of Bahir ibn Raysan, from a man among them, who said: O Messenger of Allah, a land of ours called Abyan is our land of provisions and countryside, yet it is unhealthy. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it, for part of closeness to disease is destruction. I say: It is with Abu Dawud from the hadith of Farwah ibn Musayk, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2949","19 - Chapter on ruqyah (incantations). 2478 - Ishaq said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Umarah ibn Abd, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There is no ruqyah except for that from which Sulayman took the covenant. This is a hasan mawquf hadith."
"2950","2479 - Muhammad ibn Shuayb ibn Shabur informed us, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Farwah, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, who said: I complained of an ailment and they carried me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he spent the night reciting the Quran over me as ruqyah and blowing upon me with it. This is a hadith with a weak chain."
"2951","2480 - Musaddad said: al-Mutamir narrated to us, from Rabah ibn Zayd, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complained of illness, he would blow into his hands and then wipe them over his face."
"2952","2481/1 - Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: I heard Layth narrating, from Abu Fazarah, from Said ibn Jubayr, or Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: These words are a cure for every disease: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah and all His names in general, from the evil of the poisonous and the vermin, from the evil of the harmful evil eye, from the evil of an envier when he envies, and from the evil of Ibn Qatrah and his offspring. Thirty-three angels came to their Lord and said: There is sickness in our land. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from the soil of your land and wipe your forelocks with it; it is the ruqyah of Muhammad; whoever takes a wage for it or conceals it from anyone will never prosper."
"2953","2481/2 - Al-Bazzar said: al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us, al-Mutamir narrated to us with it."
"2954","2482 - Abu Yala said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Abdillah ibn Hubayrah, from Hanash al-Sanani, from Abdillah who said: He recited in the ear of an afflicted man and he recovered, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What did you recite in his ear? He said: I recited: Did you think that We created you in vain, until the end of the surah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man of certain faith recited it over a mountain, it would move."
"2955","2483 - Abu Yala said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid, from his father, from his grandfather Ubadah, that is Ibn al-Samit, who said: I used to perform ruqyah in the pre-Islamic period for alternating fever; when Islam came I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Present it to me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ruqyah with it; there is no harm in it. By Allah, had it not been for that, I would never have performed ruqyah with it for anyone."
"2956","2484 - Musaddad said: Humayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, Ibn Umayyah narrated to us, a trustworthy one narrated to me, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, visited a sick man from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said something, so he said: Do not say like this, but say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say when he visited a sick person: O Allah, remove from him what he suffers, and reward him in what You have afflicted him with."
"2957","2485 - Abu Yala said: Musa ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Abu Attab al-Dallal narrated to us, Hafs ibn Sulayman narrated to me, Alqamah ibn Marthad narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I fell ill and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would seek refuge for me with supplications, and one day he sought refuge for me saying: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, I seek refuge for you in Allah, the One, the Eternal, who did not beget and was not begotten, and there is none like unto Him, from the evil of what you suffer. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood up to leave, he said: O Uthman, seek refuge with it, for you have never sought refuge with anything like it."
"2958","20 - Chapter on the evil eye. 2486/1 - Abu Dawud said: Talib ibn Habib narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir narrated to us, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Most of those who die from my nation, after Allah's decree, writ and predestination, die by souls, meaning by the evil eye."
"2959","2486/2 - Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us with it, saying: We know it only through this chain."
"2960","2487 - Abu Bakr said: Said ibn Shurahbil narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Abu Umayyah al-Ansari, from Ubayd ibn Rifah, from Rafi ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: One day I visited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a pot of meat was boiling with them; a piece of fat pleased me, so I took it and swallowed it, and I complained because of it for a year. Then I mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: In it were the evil eyes of seven people. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped my belly and I vomited it green. By the One who sent him with the truth, I have not complained of my stomach until this hour."
"2961","21 - Chapter on denying contagion, fleeing from the leper, and prohibiting omens. 2488 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no contagion and no omen; who infected the first?"
"2962","2489 - Al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn Zakariya narrated to us, Abdillah ibn Awn narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Usfan and there was a leper, so he hastened his pace and said: If any disease is contagious, it is this."
"2963","2490 - Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion, no omen, no Safar, and no Hamah. I said: From whom? He said: A widespread hadith. I said: What is Safar? He said: People say: A pain that seizes the belly."
"2964","2491/1 - Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and Ibn Numayr both said: al-Walid ibn Uqbah narrated to us; Uthman al-Shaybani said: Hamzah al-Zayyat narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Thalabah al-Himmani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no contagion and no Hamah, and a healthy one does not infect a sick one."
"2965","2491/2 - And Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Shuayb narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Thalabah, who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, with it."
"2966","2492 - Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Sinan, from Abu Talhah al-Khawlani, who said: While Umayr ibn Sad was among a group of the people of Palestine, and he was called the unique one of his kind, we sat on a great bench of his in his house, and he said to his slave: O boy, bring the horses to water; in the house was a trough of stone. He said: So he brought them, and said: Where is so-and-so she-camel? He said: She is mangy, dripping blood, or he said water, Ibrahim doubted. He said: Bring her to water. One of the people said: Then all the camels will become mangy. He said: Bring her; for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no contagion, no omen, and no Hamah. Have you not seen the camel in the desert that wakes with a spot on its knee or flanks that was not there before; who infected the first?"
"2967","2493 - Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Abdillah said: An omen harms none except one who believes in omens."
"2968","2494 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, Abbad narrated to us, Jafar narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An omen is only what holds you back or drives you forward."
"2969","2495/1 - Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Zama ibn Salih narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Wahram, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of us who practices magic or has it practiced for him, who believes in omens or has omens taken for him, who practices soothsaying or has it practiced for him."
"2970","2495/2 - Al-Bazzar said: Abu Musa narrated to us, Abu Amir narrated to us with it, saying: We know it only through this chain with this wording."
"2971","2496 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah, from his mother Umm Alqamah, freedwoman of Aishah, who said: I brought a young boy to Aishah for her to supplicate for him; they lifted a pillow upon which the boy was, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saw a knife under it. She said: What is this? He said: We use it against jinn and fright. She said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, took it and threw it away, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to hate omens and dislike them."
"2972","22 - Chapter on the prohibition of plucking hair from the nose. 2497 - Abu Yala said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The growth of hair in the nose is a protection from leprosy."
"2973","29 - Chapter on lancing a swelling. 2498 - Abu Yala said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi al-Samman narrated to us, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadham, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We entered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon a man of the Ansar who had a swelling, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you not remove it from him? He said: So it was lanced, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was present."
"2974","24 - Chapter on the prohibition of seeking treatment with unlawful things. 2499 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Muslim, from Masruq, who said: Abdillah said: O people, where are you being taken? Do you give your children wine to drink? Your children were born upon the fitrah (natural disposition), and Allah has not placed your cure in what He has forbidden to you."
"2975","2500 - Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from Hassan ibn Mukhariq, who said: Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: A daughter of mine fell ill, so I prepared nabidh (a soaked drink) for her in a small vessel; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while it was fermenting and said: What is this? I said: My daughter fell ill and I prepared this for her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty has not placed your cure in what is unlawful. Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"2976","25 - Chapter on the prohibition of magic. 2501 - Abu Yala said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sallam narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Whoever goes to a fortuneteller, a magician, or a soothsayer, asks him and believes him in what he says, has disbelieved in what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2977","26 - Chapter on the prohibition of looking at the stars. The hadith of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, will come, Allah willing, in the tribulations, in the story of the people of Nahrawan from the Khawarij. 27 - Chapter on soothsaying. 2502 - Abu Dawud said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Abdillah, who said: Whoever goes to a soothsayer and believes him in what he says has disbelieved in what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2978","2503/1 - And al-Masudi narrated to us, from Jami ibn Shaddad, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, who said: Abdillah said: The fortunetellers are the soothsayers of the non-Arabs; whoever believes in a soothsayer has disbelieved in what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2979","2503/2 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Jami ibn Shaddad with it."
"2980","28 - Chapter on cauterization. 2504 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Mujalid narrated to us, from Amir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man among us complained of a severe ailment, and the physicians said: He will not recover except by cauterization. His family wanted to cauterize him, but some of them said: No, until we consult the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they consulted him, and he said: No. Then the man recovered. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he said: Is this the companion of so-and-so tribe? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If this one had been cauterized, people would have said: It was the cauterization that cured him. Mujalid is weak."
"2981","2505 - Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Ziyad, who said: A woman brought her son to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whose belly was distended with fluid, and said: O Messenger of Allah, my son has been afflicted with what you see; shall I cauterize him? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not cauterize your son. So she resolved not to cauterize him. Then a camel stumbled over him and struck him, rupturing his belly, and he recovered. She returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah, I asked your permission to cauterize my son and you forbade me, then a camel passed by him and struck him, rupturing it, and he recovered. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had I permitted you, you would have claimed that the fire is what cured him."
"2982","2506 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Ibn Abjar narrated to me, from Sayyar, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Jarir, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, adjured me to be cauterized."
"2983","2507 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from a man of the Ansar of Banu Salimah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If there is healing in any of the treatments you use, it is in the incision of a cupper, a drink of honey, or a sting of fire that touches pain; and I do not love to be cauterized."
"2984","2508 - Al-Tayalisi said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from an elder, from an elder of ours whom I did not meet, who said: I entered with Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, upon Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, while he had been cauterized. He said: O Abu Abdillah, did you not know that we were forbidden this and it was disliked for us? Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The affliction became severe, and the physicians said: You have no remedy except this. Abdillah said: I would not have feared this for you."
"2985","29 - Chapter on cupping. 2509 - Musaddad said: Abdillah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Hanzalah, from Tawus, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and gave the cupper his wage."
"2986","2510 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibn Abi Tuwalah narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Hazm, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When I was taken up to heaven, I did not pass by any assembly of angels except that they said: O Muhammad, hold to cupping."
"2987","2511 - And Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about cupping and said: It is good."
"2988","2512 - Abu Yala said: Abu Mamar Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, from Said ibn Ubayd, from Ali ibn Rabiah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, I know it only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When blood is agitated in one of you, let him shed it, even with a lancet."
"2989","2513 - Abu Dawud said: Talhah narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped in the middle of his head, and he called it al-Munqidh (the rescuer)."
"2990","2514 - Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Abdillah ibn Jafar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped on his horn (side of the head) after he was poisoned."
"2991","2515 - Musaddad said: Mutamir narrated to us, from al-Sari ibn Yahya who said: I heard Muhammad ibn Sirin saying to a boy who wanted to be cupped at the beginning of the month: Do not be cupped at the beginning of the month, for cupping at the beginning of the month does not benefit."
"2992","2516 - Abu Yala said: Jubarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah al-Uqayli, from al-Husayn ibn Ali, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On Friday there is an hour in which no one is cupped except that he dies."
"2993","2517 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Umayr narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while in ihram for a pain he felt in his head."
"2994","29 - Book of righteousness and kinship. 1 - Chapter on the virtue of maintaining kinship ties. 2518 - Abu Yala said: Nafi ibn Khalid al-Tahi narrated to us, Nuh ibn Qays narrated to us, Khalid ibn Qays narrated to us, from Qatadah, from a man of Khatham, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was among a group of his Companions. I said: Are you the one who claims to be the Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which deeds are most beloved to Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith in Allah. I said: O Messenger of Allah, then what? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then maintaining kinship ties. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which deeds are most hateful to Allah Almighty? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Associating partners with Allah. I said: O Messenger of Allah, then what? He said: Severing kinship ties. I said: O Messenger of Allah, then what? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enjoining evil and forbidding good."
"2995","2519 - Al-Tayalisi said: Ishaq ibn Said narrated to us, my father narrated to us, saying: I was with Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man came to him and asked him: Who are you? And he claimed a distant kinship with him, so he softened his speech to him and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Learn your lineages so that you may maintain your kinship ties; for kinship when severed is not near even if it is close, and is not distant when maintained even if it is far. Sahih (authentic)."
"2996","2 - Chapter on warning against severing kinship ties. The hadith of Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with them both, already came in Hajj. 2520/1 - Abu Bakr said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Abu Idam al-Azdi, from Ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The angels do not descend upon a people among whom is one who severs kinship ties."
"2997","2520/2 - And Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, Abu Idam al-Azdi narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abi Awfa, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mercy does not descend upon a people among whom is one who severs kinship ties. A man from his audience said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a maternal aunt whom I have not spoken to? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand and go speak to her. I say: Abu Idam's name is Sulayman ibn Yazid al-Muharibi, and al-Bukhari narrated this hadith in al-Adab al-Mufrad. And the hadith of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, already came in the exhortation to charity in the Book of Zakah."
"2998","2521 - Abu Yala said: Ibrahim, that is Ibn al-Hajjaj, narrated to us, Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mujammi ibn Yahya al-Ansari, from Suwayd ibn Amir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Keep your kinship ties moist even with greeting. Its chain is hasan, except that it is mursal."
"2999","2522 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, al-Mundhir ibn Jahm narrated to us, from Nawfal ibn Musahiq, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Kinship is a branch attached to the mercy of the Most Merciful, pleading for its due; and He says: Will you not be pleased that I maintain whoever maintains you and sever whoever severs you; whoever maintains you has maintained Me, and whoever severs you has severed Me."
"3000","2523 - Abu Yala said: Ishaq, that is Ibn Abi Israil, narrated to us, Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydillah, from Abdillah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He said, meaning the Lord: Kinship is a branch from Me; whoever maintains it, I maintain him, and whoever severs it, I sever him."
